export const FORM_TIPS = {
  // CHEST
  'Barbell Bench Press': 'Set up with shoulder blades pinched together and a slight natural arch in your lower back. Lower the bar to the lower chest, elbows at roughly 45–75° from your torso. Drive the bar back up explosively and keep your feet flat on the floor.',
  'Incline Barbell Bench Press': 'Set the bench to 30–45° and grip the bar just outside shoulder width. Lower the bar to your upper chest, keeping your elbows at about 60° from your torso. Press upward and slightly back towards the rack to keep tension on the upper chest throughout.',
  'Decline Barbell Bench Press': 'Secure your feet under the pads and grip the bar slightly wider than shoulder width. Lower the bar to your lower chest with control, keeping your elbows tucked at around 45–60°. Press straight up and squeeze your chest at the top.',
  'Dumbbell Bench Press': 'Hold dumbbells just outside your shoulders and lower them until your elbows are level with the bench or just below. Press up in an arc, bringing the dumbbells slightly together at the top. Keep your shoulder blades retracted and your lower back with a natural arch.',
  'Incline Dumbbell Press': 'Adjust the bench to 30–45° and start with dumbbells at chest height, palms facing forward. Press up and slightly inward, fully extending your arms without locking out. Lower with control until you feel a stretch in the upper chest.',
  'Decline Dumbbell Press': 'Secure your feet, hold dumbbells at chest level with elbows out at about 45°. Press up in a slight arc towards each other. Control the descent slowly to load the chest through a full range.',
  'Cable Fly (Low to High)': 'Set the cables at the lowest position, grab one handle in each hand, and stand in a staggered stance for stability. Arc the cables upward and slightly inward until your hands meet above chest height. Feel the upper chest and front delt stretch on the way down.',
  'Cable Crossover (High to Low)': 'Set pulleys high and step forward slightly, holding a handle in each hand. Draw your hands downward and inward, finishing at hip height with hands overlapping. Keep a slight bend in your elbows and focus on squeezing the lower chest.',
  'Dumbbell Fly': 'Lie flat on the bench with dumbbells above your chest and a slight bend in your elbows. Lower the dumbbells out to the sides in a wide arc until you feel a stretch across your chest. Bring them back up along the same arc, squeezing your chest at the top.',
  'Incline Dumbbell Fly': 'Set the bench to 30–45° and perform the same wide arc motion as a flat fly. Focus on getting a deep stretch at the bottom while controlling the weight. Keep the elbows slightly bent throughout to protect your joints.',
  'Pec Deck (Machine Fly)': 'Sit with your back flat against the pad and grip the handles at chest height. Bring the pads together in a controlled arc, squeezing your chest at the centre. Let the pads open back slowly, stopping when you feel a good stretch. Do not let them slam open.',
  'Machine Chest Press': 'Adjust the seat so the handles are at mid-chest height. Press forward until your arms are nearly straight, then control the weight back until you feel a chest stretch. Keep your back pressed against the pad and avoid shrugging your shoulders.',
  'Incline Machine Press': 'Adjust the seat for an upward pressing angle so the handles line up with your upper chest. Press forward and slightly upward, extending without fully locking out. Control the return to get a full upper chest stretch.',
  'Weighted Dips (Chest)': 'Lean forward slightly and flare your elbows out to shift emphasis to the chest. Lower yourself until your upper arms are parallel to the floor or slightly below. Drive back up through your palms and squeeze the chest at the top.',
  'Push-Up': 'Place your hands just outside shoulder width and keep your body in a straight line from head to heels. Lower your chest to the floor with your elbows at about 45° from your body. Press up explosively without flaring the elbows excessively or letting your hips sag.',
  'Landmine Press': 'Hold the end of the barbell at chest height and stand in a staggered stance. Press the bar upward and forward along its natural arc, extending your arm fully. Lower with control, feeling the stretch across your chest and front delt.',
  'Smith Machine Bench Press': 'Set the bar height so it sits over your lower chest at the bottom of the movement. Use the same technique as a barbell bench press. Scapulae retracted, feet flat, controlled descent. The fixed bar path removes the need for stabilisation so focus purely on the press.',
  'Cable Fly (Neutral)': 'Set both cables at chest height and stand centred between the pulleys. With a slight bend in your elbows, draw your hands forward and together in a wide hugging arc. Hold briefly at the squeeze position before slowly returning to the stretched position.',
  'Svend Press': 'Stand holding a light plate between your palms at chest height, squeezing your hands together hard. Press the plate straight out and then pull it back towards your chest while maintaining the inward squeeze throughout. The continuous tension on the inner chest is the focus here.',
  'Cable Fly (Chest Height)': 'Set both cables at chest height and step forward slightly for tension. With elbows slightly bent, arc your hands together at chest level in front of you. Squeeze for a beat at the centre before returning with control.',
  'Dumbbell Pullover': 'Lie across or along a bench, holding a dumbbell above your chest with both hands. Lower the dumbbell back over your head in an arc until you feel a deep stretch in your chest and lats. Pull it back to the starting position, initiating with your chest rather than your arms.',

  // BACK
  'Barbell Row (Bent Over)': 'Hinge at the hips to about 45° or more, keeping a neutral spine. Pull the bar to your lower ribs or belly button, leading with your elbows and squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top. Lower with control. Avoid letting the bar drop or your torso rise on each rep.',
  'Dumbbell Row': 'Place one hand and knee on a bench for support and keep your back flat and parallel to the floor. Pull the dumbbell up towards your hip, driving your elbow behind you and squeezing the lat at the top. Lower fully to stretch the lat before pulling again.',
  'T-Bar Row': 'Straddle the bar with a slight hip hinge, keeping your chest against the pad if one is available. Drive your elbows back and squeeze your back at the top of each rep. Use a full range of motion. Lower fully to stretch before each pull.',
  'Seated Cable Row': 'Sit tall with a slight forward lean, feet braced on the platform. Pull the handle to your lower ribs or abdomen, leading with your elbows and squeezing the shoulder blades together. Let the weight pull your arms forward and your torso slightly forward to get a full lat stretch before each rep.',
  'Lat Pulldown (Wide Grip)': 'Sit with your thighs secured under the pad and lean back just slightly. Pull the bar down to upper chest level, driving your elbows towards the floor and squeezing your lats. Lower the bar with control until your arms are fully extended for a complete stretch at the top.',
  'Lat Pulldown (Close Grip)': 'Use a neutral or close grip and pull the bar down towards the top of your chest. Focus on driving your elbows down and back rather than pulling with your arms. This grip allows a longer range of motion and a deep lat stretch at the top.',
  'Pull-Up': 'Hang with a full dead-hang and pull yourself up until your chin clears the bar. Lead with your chest, not your chin, and squeeze your lats at the top. Lower slowly. Avoid swinging or kipping unless for conditioning purposes.',
  'Weighted Pull-Up': 'Attach weight via a belt or hold it between your knees and perform the same technique as a bodyweight pull-up. Control the descent especially with added weight to protect your shoulders. Full range. Dead-hang at the bottom, chin over bar at the top.',
  'Chin-Up': 'Use a supinated (underhand) grip just outside shoulder width. Pull until your chin is over the bar, keeping your elbows tracking along your sides. The supinated grip recruits the biceps more and allows a strong peak contraction at the top.',
  'Machine Row (Chest Supported)': 'Adjust the seat so your chest rests firmly against the pad and your arms are fully extended to start. Pull the handles back towards your lower ribs, squeezing the shoulder blades together. The chest support removes lower back demand. Focus entirely on the back muscles.',
  'Cable Straight-Arm Pulldown': 'Stand facing a high cable and hold a straight bar or rope with arms extended above. Keeping your arms mostly straight with just a slight elbow bend, pull the cable down to your thighs. Feel the lats working hard through the entire movement and control the return.',
  'Conventional Deadlift': 'Start with the bar over your mid-foot, hips high, and back neutral. Drive through the floor with your legs while pulling the bar close to your body. Lock out at the top by driving your hips forward. Do not hyperextend the lower back.',
  'Sumo Deadlift': 'Take a wide stance with toes turned out and grip inside your legs. Push the floor away and drive your knees out to match your toe angle as you lift. Keep the bar close to your body throughout and finish with your hips fully extended.',
  'Rack Pull': 'Set the bar just below or at knee height and approach it the same as a conventional deadlift from that point. Drive through with your legs and hips, keeping your chest tall and your spine neutral. This is great for loading the upper back and reinforcing the lockout position.',
  'Cable High Row': 'Set a cable at head or above-head height and grab the handle with a slight backward lean. Pull towards your upper chest or neck, driving your elbows downward and back. Think of trying to put your elbows in your back pockets.',
  'Single-Arm Cable Row': 'Sit or stand facing the cable and row one arm at a time with a full range of motion. Let your shoulder protract at the start to get a deep lat stretch, then retract and depress it as you pull. Avoid rotating your torso. Isolate the back.',
  'Trap Bar Deadlift': 'Stand in the centre of the trap bar and hinge to grip the handles with a neutral spine. Press the floor away with your legs and stand tall, locking your hips out at the top. The neutral grip and centred loading make this easier on the lower back than a conventional deadlift.',
  'Pendlay Row': 'The bar starts on the floor each rep. Hinge to a near-horizontal torso position and pull explosively to your lower ribs. Lower the bar back to the floor under control and reset your position each rep. The dead-stop removes momentum and keeps tension honest.',
  'Inverted Row': 'Set a barbell at hip height, hang beneath it with arms fully extended, and keep your body in a straight line. Pull your chest up to the bar by squeezing your shoulder blades together. Lower with control until your arms are straight again.',
  'Cable Lat Pullover': 'Set a cable at the highest position and face it, holding a straight bar or rope. With arms slightly bent, pull the cable down in a long arc towards your thighs, feeling your lats contract. Slowly return to the stretched position above your head.',
  'Single-Arm Lat Pulldown': 'Kneel or sit facing the cable and perform single-arm pulldowns, allowing your torso to rotate slightly for a fuller range. Pull the handle down and towards the outside of your thigh. Focus on initiating the pull with the lat, not the arm.',
  'Seal Row': 'Lie face down on an elevated bench with a barbell hanging below. Pull the bar to your sternum, squeezing your shoulder blades together. The prone position fully eliminates leg and lower-back involvement.',
  'Chest-Supported Row (Dumbbell)': 'Lie face down on an incline bench set to 45°, holding dumbbells at arm\'s length below. Row both dumbbells towards your hips, driving your elbows up and back. Full hang at the bottom for a complete lat stretch each rep.',
  'Landmine Row': 'Stand perpendicular to a landmine and grab the end of the barbell with both hands or one hand. Hinge at the hips and row the bar to your waist, driving your elbow back. The landmine arc naturally loads the lat through a good range.',
  'Seated Machine Row (Wide)': 'Use a wide overhand grip and pull the handle to your upper abdomen or lower chest. Squeeze the shoulder blades back and together at the full contraction. A wider grip and higher pull angle targets the upper back and rear delts more.',
  'Face Pull (Rope)': 'Set a rope at face height and pull towards your forehead with your elbows flaring out wide. At full contraction your hands should be beside your ears and your elbows pointing outward. Focus on external rotation of the shoulders. This is great for rear delt and rotator cuff health.',

  // SHOULDERS
  'Barbell Overhead Press': 'Stand with the bar on your upper chest, slightly in front of the clavicles. Press straight up, moving your head back slightly to allow the bar to travel in a vertical path. Lock out at the top and keep your core braced throughout.',
  'Dumbbell Shoulder Press': 'Sit upright or stand with dumbbells at ear height, palms facing forward. Press directly overhead until your arms are nearly straight, then lower back to the start with control. Keep your core tight to avoid arching your lower back excessively.',
  'Arnold Press': 'Start with dumbbells in front of your face, palms towards you, elbows low. Rotate your palms outward as you press up, finishing with a traditional overhead press. Reverse the rotation on the way down. This engages all three delt heads through the rotation.',
  'Machine Shoulder Press': 'Adjust the seat so the handles are at or just below shoulder height. Press upward until arms are nearly extended, then lower with control. Machines reduce stabiliser demand so focus on feeling the delts doing the work.',
  'Dumbbell Lateral Raise': 'Hold dumbbells at your sides with a slight bend in your elbows. Raise them out to the sides until they are parallel with the floor, leading with your elbows rather than your wrists. Lower slowly. The descent under load is just as important as the raise.',
  'Cable Lateral Raise': 'Set the cable to the lowest position, reach across your body, and raise your arm out to the side until parallel with the floor. The cable maintains constant tension throughout. Focus on a controlled lowering phase. Lean slightly away from the cable for a better range of motion.',
  'Machine Lateral Raise': 'Position the pads so they contact your forearms or elbows rather than your hands. Raise the arms out to the side in a smooth arc. Using elbows rather than hands reduces wrist and forearm involvement, keeping tension on the side delts.',
  'Dumbbell Rear Delt Fly': 'Hinge at the hips to nearly parallel, holding dumbbells with palms facing each other. Raise your arms out to the sides in a wide arc, thinking about pulling your elbows up and back. Avoid shrugging. Keep your traps relaxed and feel the rear delts working.',
  'Cable Rear Delt Fly': 'Cross the cables in front of you and grab the opposing handles. Pull the cables apart in a wide arc until your hands are just behind your body line. Focus on retracting your shoulder blades and squeezing the rear delts at the end range.',
  'Reverse Pec Deck': 'Face the machine and grab the handles in front of you, set at chest height. Reverse-fly your arms out and back, squeezing the rear delts and upper back at the end position. Avoid using your back to row the weight. Keep the motion in the arms only.',
  'Upright Row': 'Grip a barbell or dumbbells just inside shoulder width and pull upward, leading with your elbows. Raise until your elbows are just above shoulder height, then lower with control. Using a shoulder-width grip rather than narrow reduces impingement risk.',
  'Dumbbell Front Raise': 'Hold dumbbells in front of your thighs and raise one or both arms forward until shoulder height. Keep a slight bend in the elbows and avoid swinging. Lower slowly to maximise the eccentric load on the front delts.',
  'Cable Front Raise': 'Set the cable at the lowest point and hold the handle with one hand. Raise your arm forward and upward until it is at shoulder height. The cable keeps tension at the bottom where a dumbbell loses it, keeping the muscle working harder throughout.',
  'Cable Y-Raise (Prone)': 'Lie face down on a 45° incline bench facing a low cable pulley. Pull the rope or handle in a "Y" shape. Up and outward. Squeezing the rear delts and mid back at the top. Keep the movement smooth and avoid shrugging your shoulders upward.',
  'Face Pull': 'Set a rope attachment at face height on a cable. Pull the rope towards your face with your elbows flaring out wide, finishing with your hands beside your ears. This is one of the best exercises for rear delt and rotator cuff health. Prioritise control over heavy load.',
  'Dumbbell Side-Lying Rear Delt': 'Lie on your side on a bench and hold a light dumbbell with your top arm. Keeping a slight elbow bend, raise the dumbbell upward in an arc until perpendicular to the floor. Lower slowly. This position isolates the rear delt with minimal trap involvement.',
  'Seated Dumbbell Press': 'Sit upright with back support and press dumbbells from ear height straight overhead. Keep your core engaged to prevent excessive lower back arch. Use the full range. Just above shoulder height at the bottom to full extension at the top.',
  'Leaning Lateral Raise': 'Hold a fixed structure with one hand and lean away from it, creating a lengthened position for the side delt. Raise the working arm out to the side until parallel with the floor. The lean improves the stretch at the bottom and keeps the muscle under tension through the full range.',
  'Lying Rear Delt Row': 'Lie face down on a flat bench with light dumbbells hanging beneath. Row your elbows up and out to the sides, keeping your upper arms perpendicular to your torso. This strict position isolates the rear delts very effectively.',

  // BICEPS
  'Barbell Curl': 'Hold the bar with a shoulder-width supinated grip and keep your elbows pinned to your sides. Curl the bar upward in a smooth arc until your forearms are vertical, squeezing the biceps. Lower fully so your arms are straight at the bottom to get the full range.',
  'EZ Bar Curl': 'Use the angled grip to reduce wrist strain and keep your elbows tucked to your sides. Curl to the top with a strong squeeze, then lower fully without swinging. The angled grip shifts some emphasis to the brachialis alongside the biceps.',
  'Dumbbell Curl': 'Hold dumbbells with palms facing forward and curl one or both arms simultaneously. Keep your upper arms stationary and avoid letting your elbows drift forward. At the top, supinate your wrists slightly for a peak contraction.',
  'Incline Dumbbell Curl': 'Sit on an incline bench so your arms hang slightly behind your body at the start. Curl upward from this stretched position. This hits the long head of the biceps especially hard. Keep your upper arms stationary and do not swing.',
  'Hammer Curl': 'Hold dumbbells with a neutral grip (palms facing each other) and curl upward while maintaining that grip. This targets the brachialis and brachioradialis in addition to the biceps. Keep upper arms still and curl fully through the range.',
  'Cable Curl': 'Use a low cable with a straight bar or single handle. Curl with your elbows pinned and maintain constant tension thanks to the cable. The cable keeps load on the biceps at the bottom of the movement where a dumbbell would have none.',
  'Preacher Curl (EZ Bar)': 'Position your arms on the pad so the upper arms are at about 45° and fully supported. Curl from full extension to peak contraction with strict form. The preacher curl prevents cheating but be careful near full extension. The bicep tendon is vulnerable at that stretched position.',
  'Machine Curl': 'Adjust the seat so your elbows align with the machine\'s rotation axis. Curl fully to the top and lower back to a complete stretch. The machine removes the need for stabilisation so focus on squeezing and controlling the movement.',
  'Concentration Curl': 'Sit and brace your elbow against the inside of your thigh. Curl the dumbbell upward while turning your wrist slightly outward at the top. Lower fully to get the complete range of motion. This isolates the peak of the bicep very well.',
  'Cross-Body Hammer Curl': 'Curl a dumbbell across your body towards the opposite shoulder rather than straight up. Keep a neutral grip throughout. This emphasises the brachialis and gives a slightly different feel than a standard hammer curl.',
  'Zottman Curl': 'Curl up with a supinated grip, rotate to a pronated grip at the top, and lower in that position. The reverse grip on the way down trains the brachioradialis under heavy load through the eccentric. Use lighter weight than a standard curl.',
  'Spider Curl': 'Lie face down on an incline bench or use a preacher curl station, hanging your arms straight down. Curl upward to peak contraction, keeping your upper arms stationary and vertical. This position maximises bicep peak tension at the top.',
  'Cable Hammer Curl (Rope)': 'Attach a rope to a low cable, hold each end with a neutral grip. Curl upward keeping elbows close to your sides and maintaining the neutral grip at the top. Lower with control to fully extend the arms.',
  'Prone Incline Curl': 'Lie face down on an incline bench with arms hanging free. Curl upward against gravity with no opportunity to swing or cheat. This completely isolates the biceps and removes leg drive from the movement.',

  // TRICEPS
  'Close-Grip Bench Press': 'Take a grip roughly shoulder-width (narrower than regular bench), keeping your elbows closer to your sides. Lower the bar to your lower chest and press straight up. This is a compound movement that heavily loads all three tricep heads.',
  'EZ Bar Skull Crusher': 'Lie flat and hold the EZ bar with a narrow grip, arms vertical over your chest. Keeping your upper arms still, lower the bar towards your forehead by bending your elbows. Press back up by straightening the elbows. Do not let the upper arms drift backward.',
  'Cable Pushdown (Straight Bar)': 'Stand facing a high cable and grip the bar with elbows tucked to your sides. Push the bar downward to full arm extension, squeezing the triceps hard. Control the return. Do not let the weight yank your elbows upward.',
  'Rope Pushdown': 'Use a rope attachment on a high cable and grip each end. Push down and spread the rope slightly at full extension to get a wider tricep squeeze. Control the weight on the way back up, getting a good stretch at the top.',
  'Overhead Cable Tricep Extension': 'Turn away from or face a high cable and hold the rope or bar overhead. Extend your arms fully from the bent position, feeling a deep stretch in the long head of the tricep. The overhead position maximises long head recruitment.',
  'Overhead Dumbbell Extension': 'Hold one dumbbell with both hands and extend it overhead. Lower behind your head by bending your elbows, keeping your upper arms close to your ears. Extend fully and feel the long head stretch at the bottom.',
  'Weighted Dips (Triceps)': 'Keep your torso upright and elbows close to your body to emphasise the triceps. Lower until your upper arms are parallel to the floor, then drive back up. Avoid leaning forward excessively. That shifts emphasis to the chest.',
  'Machine Tricep Extension': 'Adjust the seat and align your elbows with the machine pivot. Push through the full range from bent to fully extended arms. Lower with control and feel the tricep stretch before the next rep.',
  'JM Press': 'Lie on a bench with a close grip and lower the bar towards your neck, allowing the elbows to flare slightly. This is a hybrid between a skull crusher and a close-grip press, heavily loading the triceps. Lower under control to avoid stress on the elbow joints.',
  'Tricep Kickback': 'Hinge forward at the hip with a dumbbell in one hand, upper arm parallel to the floor. Extend the forearm back until the arm is straight, squeezing the tricep at lockout. Lower with control. Avoid swinging the weight.',
  'Diamond Push-Up': 'Place your hands together beneath your chest forming a diamond shape. Lower your chest towards your hands, keeping elbows close to your body. Press back up to full arm extension. This bodyweight push-up variation strongly targets the triceps.',
  'Tate Press': 'Lie flat holding dumbbells above your chest with elbows flared outward. Lower the dumbbells inward by bending the elbows until they nearly touch your chest. Press back up by extending the elbows, keeping them flared throughout.',
  'Lying Tricep Extension': 'Lie on a flat bench holding dumbbells with arms fully extended above your chest. Bend only at the elbows, lowering the dumbbells towards the sides of your head. Extend back to the starting position without moving the upper arms.',
  'Decline Skull Crusher': 'Lie on a decline bench and hold an EZ bar or dumbbells overhead. Lower towards the forehead just as with a flat skull crusher. The decline angle shifts emphasis slightly. Keep upper arms vertical and press back up by extending the elbows only.',
  'Bench Dip': 'Place your hands on a bench behind you and feet on the floor or elevated. Lower your body by bending your elbows to about 90°, then press back up. Keep your torso close to the bench to target the triceps rather than the front delts.',

  // QUADS
  'Barbell Back Squat': 'Place the bar on your upper traps (high bar) or rear delts (low bar), brace your core, and squat until your thighs are at least parallel to the floor. Keep your chest tall, push your knees out to match your toes, and drive up through your whole foot. Breathe in at the top, hold through the bottom, exhale on the way up.',
  'Barbell Front Squat': 'Hold the bar in a clean grip or crossed-arm position on your front delts, elbows high. Keep your torso upright as you squat. The front-loaded position demands a very upright back. Squat to parallel or below, knees tracking over toes.',
  'Leg Press': 'Place your feet at mid-height on the platform, about shoulder width. Lower the sled until your knees are at 90° or slightly more, then press back up without locking out your knees. A higher foot placement targets the glutes more; lower targets the quads more.',
  'Hack Squat Machine': 'Position your shoulders under the pads and feet mid-platform, shoulder width apart. Lower until thighs are parallel or below, keeping your back against the pad. Drive up through your full foot, not just the toes.',
  'Bulgarian Split Squat': 'Elevate your rear foot on a bench and stand far enough in front that your front knee tracks over your toes at the bottom. Lower until your rear knee nearly touches the floor, keeping your front shin as vertical as possible. Drive up through your front heel.',
  'Dumbbell Lunge': 'Step forward and lower your back knee towards the ground, keeping your front shin relatively vertical. Push off the front foot to return to standing. Keep your torso upright and avoid letting your front knee cave inward.',
  'Walking Lunge': 'Step forward into a lunge position, lower the back knee towards the floor, then drive off the front foot to step into the next lunge. Maintain an upright torso throughout. This challenges balance and coordination alongside quad and glute strength.',
  'Leg Extension': 'Sit with your knees aligned with the machine pivot and push the pad upward to full knee extension. Squeeze the quads hard at the top. Lower slowly. The eccentric phase under load is highly effective for quad development.',
  'Goblet Squat': 'Hold a dumbbell or kettlebell at your chest and squat deep, using your elbows to push your knees outward at the bottom. Keep your chest tall and heels on the floor. Great for learning proper squat mechanics before loading a barbell.',
  'Smith Machine Squat': 'Position the bar on your upper traps and set up as you would for a barbell squat. The fixed bar path allows you to push your feet slightly further forward. Lower to parallel or below and drive straight up.',
  'Sumo Squat': 'Take a wide stance with toes pointing out 30–45°. Squat down while pushing knees out hard to match toe direction. Keep your chest tall and back flat throughout. This wide stance shifts more load to the inner thighs and glutes.',
  'Leg Press (Narrow Stance)': 'Place your feet close together and low on the platform to emphasise the outer quads. Lower until your knees nearly reach your chest, then press back out without locking. Narrow stance increases the demand on the rectus femoris (outer quad sweep).',
  'Sissy Squat': 'Stand with heels elevated or holding a support, and lean your torso backward while dropping your knees towards the floor. Your hips should remain aligned with your knees and ankles as you descend. This isolates the rectus femoris heavily. Ease into it as it can stress the knees.',
  'Wall Sit': 'Stand with your back flat against a wall and lower until your thighs are parallel to the floor, knees over your feet. Hold this position for time, keeping your back flat and core engaged. This is a static quad endurance exercise. Push the duration as your strength improves.',
  'Pendulum Squat': 'Load the machine and position yourself with the pad against your upper back. Squat with an upright torso, allowing the machine to guide the path. Drive up through your whole foot and squeeze the quads at the top.',

  // HAMSTRINGS
  'Romanian Deadlift (Barbell)': 'Hold a barbell at hip height with a shoulder-width grip. Hinge at the hips, pushing them back while lowering the bar along your legs. Feel a deep stretch in your hamstrings at the bottom before driving your hips forward to return to standing.',
  'Romanian Deadlift (Dumbbell)': 'Hold dumbbells in front of your thighs and hinge at the hips with a flat back. Lower the dumbbells along your legs until you feel a hamstring stretch, then drive your hips forward. Keeping the dumbbells close to your legs reduces lower back strain.',
  'Lying Leg Curl': 'Lie face down and position the pad against the back of your lower calves. Curl your heels towards your glutes in a smooth arc. Squeeze hard at the peak and lower slowly. An exaggerated slow eccentric will build more hamstring muscle.',
  'Seated Leg Curl': 'Adjust the thigh pad so it rests firmly on your quads and seat height so your knees align with the pivot. Curl your feet underneath the seat to full flexion and squeeze. Lower slowly back to the starting position without letting the weight stack bounce.',
  'Standing Leg Curl': 'Position the pad above your ankle and hold the support handles for stability. Curl one leg at a time, bringing your heel towards your glute. Full range of motion. Fully extend between reps to maximise the stretch.',
  'Nordic Hamstring Curl': 'Kneel with your feet anchored, arms crossed on your chest. Slowly lower your torso towards the floor under control, resisting with your hamstrings as long as possible. Catch yourself with your hands near the floor if needed, then push back to the top. This is extremely challenging. Progress the range gradually.',
  'Good Morning': 'Place the barbell on your upper traps and keep a slight knee bend. Hinge at the hips, lowering your torso until it is near horizontal while maintaining a neutral spine. Drive your hips forward to return upright. This is the hip hinge pattern. Prioritise back position over depth.',
  'Stiff-Leg Deadlift': 'Similar to the Romanian deadlift but with legs nearly straight, allowing a deeper hip hinge and more hamstring stretch. Keep the bar close to your legs and your back flat throughout. Do not round the lower back to achieve more range.',
  'Kettlebell Swing': 'Start with the kettlebell between your feet and hike it back through your legs. Drive your hips forward explosively to swing the bell to chest height, then hinge back as it descends. The power comes from the hip snap. This is a hip hinge, not a squat.',
  'Single-Leg Romanian Deadlift': 'Hinge forward on one leg while the other extends behind you for balance. Keep your hips square to the floor and your back flat. Lower until you feel a hamstring stretch, then drive through the standing heel to return. Use a wall or support until you have the balance.',
  'Swiss Ball Leg Curl': 'Lie on your back with your heels on a stability ball, hips off the floor in a bridge. Roll the ball towards your glutes by bending your knees, then straighten your legs back out. Keep your hips elevated throughout. Do not let them drop as the ball rolls away.',

  // GLUTES
  'Barbell Hip Thrust': 'Rest your upper back against a bench, roll a barbell over your hips with a pad for comfort, and plant your feet hip-width apart. Drive your hips upward until your thighs are parallel to the floor, squeezing your glutes hard at the top. Lower with control. Do not let your lower back do the work.',
  'Dumbbell Hip Thrust': 'Place a dumbbell on your hips and perform the same hip thrust pattern. Hold the dumbbell in place with both hands and focus on driving through the heels. Squeeze and hold the glutes at the top for a second.',
  'Cable Kickback': 'Attach an ankle strap, face the cable pulley, and kick one leg back and upward while keeping it mostly straight. Squeeze the glute hard at full extension. Avoid rotating your torso. Keep the movement isolated to the hip.',
  'Cable Pull-Through': 'Face away from a low cable, legs straddled over the cable, and hold the rope between your legs. Hinge forward at the hips with a neutral spine, then drive your hips forward to stand. This trains the hip hinge pattern with glute emphasis, similar to a light deadlift.',
  'Glute Bridge': 'Lie on your back with knees bent and feet flat on the floor. Drive your hips up by squeezing your glutes until your body forms a straight line from knees to shoulders. Hold at the top for a beat, then lower slowly. Adding a band above the knees increases glute activation.',
  'Step-Up (Dumbbell)': 'Step onto a box or bench with one foot, driving through your heel to lift your body up. Bring the other foot up to meet it, then step back down. The working leg does all the lifting. Avoid pushing off the back foot for assistance.',
  'Abductor Machine': 'Sit with the pads against the outsides of your knees and push outward. Squeeze the glutes and hip abductors at the fully open position. Avoid using momentum. Slow and controlled beats heavy and fast for this exercise.',
  'Reverse Hyperextension': 'Lie face down with your hips at the edge of the machine and hold the handles. Raise your legs behind you by contracting your glutes, keeping a slight knee bend. Lower with control to get the stretch before the next rep.',
  'Cable Hip Abduction': 'Attach an ankle strap to a low cable and stand perpendicular to the pulley. Lift the outside leg out to the side against the cable resistance. Squeeze the glute and abductor at the top, then lower with control. Keep your standing leg slightly bent and hold a support for balance.',
  '45-Degree Hip Extension': 'Position yourself in the 45° hyperextension machine with the pads across your hips. Hinge forward at the hips with a neutral spine, then drive your hips up to the starting position by squeezing your glutes. This can target the glutes or hamstrings depending on rounding vs. Flat back.',
  'Sumo Deadlift (High Bar)': 'Similar to the conventional sumo deadlift but with a more upright torso. Wide stance, toes out, grip inside the legs. Push your knees out hard throughout the lift. Drive through the floor, keeping the bar close to your legs the entire way up.',
  'Smith Machine Hip Thrust': 'Set the bar on the Smith machine at the appropriate height for the top of the thrust. Use a bench to support your upper back and drive your hips up into the bar. The fixed bar path allows you to focus entirely on glute force rather than bar balance.',

  // CALVES
  'Standing Calf Raise (Machine)': 'Position the pads on your shoulders and stand with the balls of your feet on the edge of the platform. Lower your heels as far as comfortable for a full stretch, then rise up onto the tips of your toes. Hold at the top briefly, then lower slowly. Calves respond well to full range of motion and slower tempos.',
  'Seated Calf Raise': 'Sit with the pads across your lower thighs, just above the knees, and feet on the platform edge. Lower your heels for a full stretch, then press your toes down to raise the heels as high as possible. The seated position targets the soleus (deep calf) more than the standing variation.',
  'Leg Press Calf Raise': 'Use the leg press machine with just the balls of your feet on the bottom edge of the platform. Perform a full range of motion. Deep stretch at the bottom to full plantarflexion at the top. Avoid locking your knees. Keep a slight bend throughout.',
  'Smith Machine Calf Raise': 'Place your feet at the front edge of the calf block and perform raises with the Smith bar across your shoulders. Full range of motion is key. Deep stretch followed by a strong peak contraction. The Smith machine allows more load than many dedicated machines.',
  'Single-Leg Calf Raise (Bodyweight)': 'Hold a support for balance and perform single-leg raises standing on the edge of a step for full range. Sink the heel low, then rise fully onto the tip of your big toe. Single-leg removes the assistance of the other leg, making this a serious challenge even with bodyweight.',
  'Donkey Calf Raise': 'Hinge forward at the hips with your forearms on a support, positioning your weight across your lower back or hips. Perform full range calf raises from this hinged position. The hip flexion angle changes the stretch pattern slightly, targeting the gastrocnemius through a slightly longer range.',
  'Dumbbell Calf Raise (Standing)': 'Hold dumbbells for resistance and stand on a step or flat floor. Perform full range raises. Deep heel drop and strong peak contraction. This is a simple option when machines are not available.',
  'Box Jump': 'Stand in front of a plyo box, bend into a quarter squat, and swing your arms to generate momentum. Jump explosively, landing softly on the box in a slight squat position. Step back down rather than jumping down to reduce impact stress on your joints.',

  // ABS
  'Cable Crunch': 'Kneel facing a high cable with a rope attachment and hold the rope by your head. Flex your spine. Crunch your ribcage towards your pelvis. Rather than just pulling the weight down with your arms. Hold the peak contraction briefly before returning.',
  'Hanging Leg Raise': 'Hang from a pull-up bar with your arms fully extended. Raise your legs upward, either straight or with bent knees, until they are parallel to the floor or higher. Lower with control. Avoid swinging or using momentum.',
  'Plank': 'Forearms on the floor, elbows under your shoulders, and body in a straight line from head to heels. Brace your core hard as if you are about to take a punch, and squeeze your glutes. Do not let your hips sag or pike up. Hold a rigid, straight position throughout.',
  'Ab Rollout': 'Kneel with hands on the ab wheel, core braced. Roll forward slowly, extending your body as far as you can without your lower back sagging. Roll back in by contracting your abs. This is very demanding so start with a limited range until you build strength.',
  'Decline Crunch': 'Anchor your feet on a decline bench and lie back. Crunch your ribcage towards your pelvis rather than pulling your head up. Lower all the way back under control to get a full stretch of the abs.',
  'Russian Twist': 'Sit on the floor with knees slightly bent and torso leaning back at about 45°. Rotate your torso left and right, touching the floor or a weight beside your hips. Keep your lower back neutral. Avoid rounding. Add a weight plate to increase difficulty.',
  'Pallof Press': 'Set a cable at chest height and stand perpendicular to it. Hold the handle with both hands at your chest, then press straight out and hold, resisting the cable\'s pull to rotate you. This is an anti-rotation exercise. The goal is to stay perfectly still through your core.',
  'Landmine Twist': 'Hold the end of a barbell with both hands and arc it from side to side in front of your body. The landmine loads the rotation through the full range. Keep your hips forward and rotate through the thoracic spine.',
  'Side Plank': 'Lie on your side with one forearm on the floor under your shoulder and feet stacked. Raise your hips so your body forms a straight line. Hold the position by bracing through your obliques. Do not let your hip sag downward.',
  'Bicycle Crunch': 'Lie on your back with hands loosely behind your head. Alternately bring one elbow to the opposite knee while extending the other leg. Keep the movement slow and deliberate. Do not rush or simply rock from side to side.',
  'V-Up': 'Lie flat on your back with arms overhead. Simultaneously raise your legs and torso, reaching your hands towards your feet at the top. Lower back with control. Keep your core braced throughout. This is a full-body core compression exercise.',
  'Sit-Up': 'Lie with knees bent and feet on the floor. Rise until your torso is upright, then lower back slowly. Avoid pulling on your neck with your hands. Cross your arms on your chest or hold them lightly behind your head without yanking.',
  'Leg Raise (Flat Bench)': 'Lie on a flat bench, holding the edges for support. Lower your legs towards the floor, keeping them straight or slightly bent, then raise them back to horizontal. This is gentler on the hands than hanging leg raises but still very effective.',
  'Dragon Flag': 'Lie on a bench holding the top with both hands. Raise your entire body in a straight line, supported only by your upper back and arms. Lower your body slowly with complete control. This is an extremely advanced core exercise that requires significant preparation.',
  'Cable Woodchop': 'Set a cable at one side. Either high or low. And use both hands to pull and rotate across your body. Rotate through your thoracic spine with your hips relatively stable. Control the return. Do not let the cable yank you back.',

  // TRAPS
  'Barbell Shrug': 'Hold the barbell in front of your thighs with a shoulder-width grip. Shrug straight up. Raise your shoulders towards your ears. Pause at the top, squeeze, then lower fully. Do not roll your shoulders; straight up and down is more effective and safer.',
  'Dumbbell Shrug': 'Hold dumbbells at your sides and shrug straight up, elevating your shoulders as high as possible. Lower fully between reps for a good stretch at the bottom. Avoid rolling your shoulders. The traps respond to pure elevation.',
  'Cable Shrug': 'Use a straight bar or rope on a low cable and shrug upward. The cable provides constant tension even at the bottom, making it effective throughout the full range. Hold briefly at the top contraction.',
  "Farmer's Walk": 'Pick up heavy dumbbells or plates with a neutral grip and walk with an upright posture, not allowing the weights to pull your shoulders down. Walk a set distance or for time, bracing your core and keeping a tall spine. This builds trap endurance and grip strength simultaneously.',
  'Hex Bar Shrug': 'Stand inside the hex bar and grip the handles at your sides. Shrug straight upward just as you would with dumbbells. The neutral grip and central loading make this more comfortable for heavy shrugs.',
  'Smith Machine Shrug': 'Set the bar at hip height and stand inside the Smith machine. Grip the bar and shrug upward with full elevation. The guided bar path removes the need to balance the load laterally.',
  'Behind-the-Back Barbell Shrug': 'Hold the barbell behind your body at arm\'s length, resting against your glutes. Shrug straight upward. This angle can feel more natural for some people and emphasises the upper trap slightly differently than the front hold.',

  // FOREARMS
  'Barbell Wrist Curl': 'Sit on a bench and rest your forearms on your thighs with wrists hanging off the edge. Curl your wrists upward against the weight. Lower fully to get a stretch before each rep. Use a lighter weight and focus on the pump.',
  'Reverse Wrist Curl': 'The same as a wrist curl but with a pronated (overhand) grip. Extend your wrists upward, working the wrist extensors on the top of the forearm. These are usually much weaker than the flexors, so use very light weight.',
  'Reverse Curl': 'Perform a standard curl but with an overhand grip, working the brachioradialis and wrist extensors alongside the biceps. Keep the elbows pinned and curl through the full range. Lower slowly.',
  'Dead Hang': 'Hang from a pull-up bar with your hands fully open or closed. Simply hold your bodyweight for time. This builds grip strength and decompresses the spine. Gradually increase your hang time as your grip gets stronger.',
  'Dumbbell Wrist Curl': 'Sit and rest one forearm on your thigh with your palm facing up. Curl the dumbbell upward using just your wrist, lower fully. This allows a greater range of motion than a barbell version.',
  'Plate Pinch': 'Pick up one or two plates by pinching them between your fingers and thumb (smooth side out). Hold for time or walk with them. The goal is to maintain the pinch grip. This directly targets the finger flexors and overall grip.',
  'Cable Reverse Curl': 'Use a straight bar on a low cable with an overhand grip. Curl the bar upward just as with a regular curl. The cable maintains tension at the bottom where a barbell would have none, making it more effective for the brachioradialis.',

  // ── Full-library coverage: tips for every remaining exercise ──
  'Decline Dumbbell Fly': 'Secure your feet under the pads and hold the dumbbells above your lower chest with a slight bend in your elbows. Lower them out to the sides in a wide arc until you feel a deep stretch across the chest. Bring them back along the same arc and squeeze the lower chest at the top. Keep the elbow bend fixed throughout to protect the joints.',
  'Decline Machine Press': 'Adjust the seat so the handles line up with your lower chest on a downward pressing angle. Press forward and slightly down, extending without locking out. Control the return until you feel a chest stretch, keeping your back against the pad and shoulders down.',
  'Wide-Grip Push-Up': 'Place your hands wider than shoulder width and keep your body in a straight line from head to heels. Lower your chest towards the floor, letting your elbows travel out at around 45 to 60 degrees. The wider hand position increases the chest stretch, so press up under control and do not let your hips sag.',
  'Decline Push-Up': 'Place your feet on a bench or box with your hands on the floor just outside shoulder width. Keep a straight line from head to heels and lower your chest under control, elbows at about 45 degrees. The elevated feet shift more load to the upper chest and front delts, so press up without letting the hips drop.',
  'Ring Push-Up': 'Grip the rings just outside shoulder width and set your body in a straight line, bracing your core to control the wobble. Lower your chest between the rings, keeping your elbows at around 45 degrees, then press up and turn the rings out slightly at the top. The instability recruits the chest stabilisers hard, so prioritise control over speed.',
  'Incline Smith Machine Press': 'Set the bench to 30 to 45 degrees with the bar lined up over your upper chest. Retract your shoulder blades, plant your feet, and lower the bar to your upper chest under control. The fixed bar path removes the need to stabilise, so press up and slightly back and focus purely on the upper chest.',
  'High Cable Fly': 'Set both pulleys high and step forward slightly with a staggered stance for stability. With a slight bend in your elbows, draw your hands down and together in a wide arc, finishing around hip height with your hands overlapping. Squeeze the lower chest at the bottom and return with control to feel the stretch.',
  'Low Cable Fly': 'Set both pulleys to the lowest position and stand centred with a handle in each hand. Keeping a slight elbow bend, arc your hands upward and inward until they meet above chest height. Squeeze the upper chest at the top, then lower along the same arc to feel the stretch across the chest and front delt.',
  'Incline Cable Fly': 'Set an adjustable bench to 30 to 45 degrees between two low or mid pulleys. With a slight bend in your elbows, arc the handles up and together over your upper chest, then lower out to the sides for a deep stretch. The cables keep constant tension on the upper chest throughout the range.',
  'Hammer Strength Chest Press': 'Adjust the seat so the handles sit at mid-chest height and keep your back firmly against the pad. Press the handles forward until your arms are nearly straight, then control the return until you feel a chest stretch. The independent arms let each side work through its full range, so do not shrug your shoulders.',
  'Reverse-Grip Bench Press': 'Set up with shoulder blades retracted and grip the bar with a supinated (underhand) grip at roughly shoulder width. Lower the bar to your lower chest with your elbows tucked close to your sides, then press straight up. The reverse grip shifts emphasis to the upper chest, so use a spotter as the grip can feel less stable.',
  'Dumbbell Squeeze Press': 'Lie flat holding two dumbbells pressed firmly together over your chest, palms facing each other. Lower them to your chest while pushing them hard against each other, then press straight back up maintaining that inward squeeze. The constant pressure between the dumbbells keeps tension on the inner chest throughout.',
  'Cable Fly (High to Low)': 'Set both pulleys high and step forward slightly with a staggered stance. Keeping a slight bend in your elbows, draw your hands down and together in a wide arc, finishing around hip height with your hands overlapping. Squeeze the lower chest at the bottom, then return with control to feel the stretch.',
  'Dumbbell Pullover (Chest)': 'Lie across or along a bench holding a dumbbell above your chest with both hands. Lower it back over your head in an arc until you feel a deep stretch across the chest, keeping a slight bend in your elbows. Pull it back over your chest by initiating with the chest rather than the arms, and keep your hips down to maximise the stretch.',
  'Barbell Row (Supinated)': 'Hinge at the hips to around 45 degrees with a neutral spine and grip the bar with a shoulder-width underhand grip. Pull the bar to your lower abdomen, leading with your elbows and squeezing your shoulder blades together. The supinated grip recruits the lower lats and biceps more. Lower with control and avoid letting your torso rise on each rep.',
  'Chest-Supported T-Bar Row': 'Lie chest-down against the pad with your feet planted and grip the handles with arms fully extended. Pull the handles back towards your hips, driving your elbows up and back and squeezing your shoulder blades together. The chest support removes lower back demand, so let your arms fully extend at the bottom for a complete lat stretch.',
  'Lat Pulldown (Neutral Grip)': 'Use a neutral (palms-facing) handle, secure your thighs under the pad, and lean back just slightly. Pull the handle down to your upper chest, driving your elbows down and back rather than pulling with your arms. The neutral grip allows a strong contraction and a long range, so let your arms fully extend at the top for a complete stretch.',
  'Neutral Grip Pull-Up': 'Hang from parallel (palms-facing) handles in a full dead-hang. Pull yourself up by driving your elbows down towards your ribs until your chin clears the handles, leading with your chest. The neutral grip is easier on the shoulders and recruits the lats and brachialis well. Lower slowly and avoid swinging.',
  'Assisted Pull-Up': 'Set the assistance weight on the machine and place your knees or feet on the pad, then grip the bar at roughly shoulder width. Pull yourself up until your chin clears the bar, driving your elbows down and squeezing your lats. Lower under control to a full dead-hang. Reduce the assistance over time as you get stronger.',
  'Snatch Grip Deadlift': 'Take a very wide (snatch-width) grip with the bar over your mid-foot and your hips set low, back neutral. Drive through the floor with your legs while keeping the bar close to your body. The wide grip increases the range of motion and loads the upper back hard, so keep your chest tall and do not round the lower back.',
  'Deficit Deadlift': 'Stand on a low platform of 2 to 4 inches with the bar over your mid-foot, hips set, and back neutral. Drive through the floor and pull the bar close to your body, locking out by driving your hips forward. The deficit increases the range of motion off the floor, so reduce the load and keep your spine neutral throughout.',
  'Meadows Row': 'Stand perpendicular to a landmine and grip the end of the bar with one hand, hinging forward with a flat back. Pull the bar up towards your hip, driving your elbow high and back and letting your shoulder blade move freely. Lower fully to stretch the lat, keeping your torso braced and avoiding rotation through the spine.',
  'Kroc Row': 'Brace one hand on a rack or bench and hold a heavy dumbbell in the other, hinged forward with a flat back. Row the dumbbell explosively up towards your hip, allowing a small amount of controlled body movement to shift heavy loads. This is a high-rep heavy back builder, so keep your spine neutral and control the lowering on every rep.',
  'Helms Row': 'Set an incline bench to a steep angle and rest your chest against it, holding a dumbbell in each hand. Pull the dumbbells towards your hips with a moderate elbow angle, focusing on a strict squeeze of the back. The chest support keeps the lower back out of it, so use a controlled tempo rather than heavy momentum.',
  'Hyperextension (Back Extension)': 'Position yourself in the 45 or 90 degree bench with the pads across your hips and your feet anchored. Hinge forward at the hips with a neutral spine, then raise your torso back in line with your body by squeezing your glutes and lower back. Do not hyperextend past a straight line at the top.',
  'Good Morning (Barbell)': 'Place the barbell on your upper traps and keep a slight bend in your knees. Hinge at the hips, pushing them back and lowering your torso towards horizontal while keeping a neutral spine. Drive your hips forward to return upright. This is a hip hinge, so prioritise back position over depth and start light.',
  'Chest-Supported Row (Barbell)': 'Lie face down on an incline bench with a barbell hanging beneath you. Pull the bar up to your lower chest or upper abdomen, squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top. The prone position removes any leg drive or lower back involvement, so fully extend your arms at the bottom for a complete stretch.',
  'Cable Row (Wide Grip)': 'Sit tall with a slight forward lean and grip a wide bar with an overhand grip. Pull the bar to your upper abdomen, driving your elbows out and back and squeezing your shoulder blades together. The wide grip targets the upper back and rear delts more, so let your arms extend forward for a full stretch before each rep.',
  'Machine Row (Hammer Strength)': 'Adjust the seat so the handles sit at mid-torso height and rest your chest against the pad. Pull the handles back towards your ribs, driving your elbows back and squeezing your shoulder blades together. The independent arms let each side work fully, so let your arms extend forward at the start for a complete lat stretch.',
  'TRX Row': 'Grip the TRX handles and walk your feet forward so your body leans back in a straight line, the lower the angle the harder it is. Pull your chest up towards the handles by driving your elbows back and squeezing your shoulder blades together. Keep your body rigid from head to heels and lower under control until your arms are straight.',
  'Back Extension (Weighted)': 'Position yourself in the hyperextension bench with the pads across your hips and hold a plate against your chest. Hinge forward at the hips with a neutral spine, then raise your torso back in line with your body by squeezing your glutes and lower back. Do not hyperextend past a straight line at the top, and keep the load close to your chest.',
  'Z-Press': 'Sit on the floor with your legs straight out in front and the bar at your upper chest. Press the bar straight overhead, locking out with your head moving slightly back as the bar passes. The seated floor position removes all leg drive and forces a tall, braced torso, so keep your core tight and avoid leaning back.',
  'Push Press': 'Start with the bar on your upper chest and your feet hip-width apart. Dip slightly at the knees, then drive explosively through your legs to launch the bar off your shoulders, finishing the press with your arms overhead. Lock out with your head through the bar and reset to the shoulders under control before the next rep.',
  'Landmine Lateral Raise': 'Stand beside a landmine and grip the end of the bar with the hand furthest from it across your body. Raise the bar out and up in an arc to about shoulder height, leading with your elbow. The landmine arc keeps tension on the side delt through the range, so lower with control and avoid shrugging.',
  'Seated Lateral Raise': 'Sit upright on a bench holding dumbbells at your sides with a slight bend in your elbows. Raise them out to the sides until parallel with the floor, leading with your elbows rather than your wrists. The seated position removes any leg drive or swing, so lower slowly and keep the side delts under tension.',
  'Viking Press': 'Set up at a landmine or Viking press attachment and grip the handles at shoulder height with a neutral grip. Press up and slightly forward along the natural arc until your arms are extended, then lower under control to your shoulders. Brace your core and avoid leaning back to push the weight.',
  'Leaning Cable Lateral Raise': 'Hold the upright with one hand and lean away from a low cable, holding the handle in your working hand across your body. Raise your arm out to the side until parallel with the floor, leading with your elbow. The lean lengthens the side delt at the bottom and keeps tension through the full range, so lower slowly.',
  'Plate Lateral Raise': 'Hold a weight plate in each hand, or one in front, with a slight bend in your elbows at your sides. Raise the plates out to the sides until parallel with the floor, leading with your elbows. Lower slowly under control, keeping your traps relaxed so the side delts do the work.',
  'Cable Upright Row': 'Set a straight bar or rope on a low cable and grip just inside shoulder width. Pull upward, leading with your elbows, until they reach just above shoulder height, then lower with control. The cable keeps constant tension, and a shoulder-width grip reduces impingement risk, so do not pull the elbows too high.',
  'Dumbbell Y-Raise': 'Hold light dumbbells with a neutral grip and a slight forward lean at the hips. Raise your arms up and out in a Y shape to about head height, thumbs pointing up, squeezing the rear delts and lower traps. Keep the movement smooth and avoid shrugging your shoulders towards your ears.',
  'Machine Rear Delt Fly': 'Sit facing the pad and grip the handles set at chest height with your arms extended in front. Open your arms back and out in a wide arc, squeezing the rear delts and upper back at the end range. Keep the motion in your arms only and avoid using your back to row the weight.',
  'W-Raise': 'Hold light dumbbells with a slight forward lean, elbows bent and tucked towards your sides. Pull your arms up and back into a W shape by driving your elbows down and squeezing your shoulder blades together with external rotation. This targets the rear delts and rotator cuff, so keep the load light and the movement controlled.',
  'YTW': 'Lie face down on an incline bench holding light dumbbells and perform three positions in sequence, the Y up and out overhead, the T straight out to the sides, and the W with elbows bent and pulled back. Squeeze the rear delts and mid back in each position. Keep the weights light and avoid shrugging throughout.',
  'Cable Face Pull': 'Set a rope or handle at face height and pull towards your forehead with your elbows flaring out wide. At full contraction your hands should finish beside your ears with your elbows pointing outward. Focus on external rotation of the shoulders. This is excellent for rear delt and rotator cuff health, so prioritise control over load.',
  'Cable Face Pull (Rope)': 'Attach a rope at face height and grip an end in each hand with your thumbs pointing back. Pull towards your forehead, splitting the rope apart and flaring your elbows out wide until your hands finish beside your ears. Focus on external rotation and squeezing the rear delts, keeping the load light and controlled.',
  'Bent-Over Cable Rear Delt Fly': 'Hinge forward at the hips with a flat back, holding a low cable handle in the opposite hand across your body. Sweep your arm out to the side in a wide arc until level with your torso, squeezing the rear delt. Keep your elbow slightly bent and avoid using your back to swing the weight.',
  'Prone Incline Y-Raise': 'Lie face down on an incline bench holding light dumbbells with a neutral grip. Raise your arms up and out in a Y shape to about head height, thumbs pointing up, squeezing the rear delts and lower traps. The prone position removes all momentum, so keep the load light and avoid shrugging.',
  'Prone Incline T-Raise': 'Lie face down on an incline bench holding light dumbbells. Raise your arms straight out to the sides in a T shape until level with your shoulders, squeezing your shoulder blades together. The prone position isolates the rear delts and mid traps, so keep the movement strict and avoid swinging.',
  'Seated Rear Delt Machine': 'Sit facing the pad and grip the handles at chest height with your arms extended in front. Open your arms back and out in a wide arc, squeezing the rear delts and upper back at the end position. Keep the motion isolated to your arms and avoid rowing with your back. Lower with control.',
  'Preacher Curl (Barbell)': 'Rest your upper arms flat on the preacher pad at about 45 degrees and grip the barbell with a shoulder-width supinated grip. Curl from full extension to peak contraction with strict form, then lower under control. Be careful near full extension as the bicep tendon is vulnerable in that stretched position, so do not bounce out of the bottom.',
  'Preacher Curl (Dumbbell)': 'Rest one upper arm flat on the preacher pad and hold a dumbbell with a supinated grip. Curl from full extension to peak contraction, squeezing the bicep at the top, then lower under control. Work one arm at a time for full focus, and avoid jerking out of the stretched bottom position to protect the tendon.',
  'EZ Bar Preacher Curl': 'Rest your upper arms flat on the preacher pad and grip the EZ bar on its angled sections to reduce wrist strain. Curl from full extension to peak contraction with strict form, then lower under control. The preacher pad prevents cheating, so be careful at the bottom as the bicep tendon is vulnerable in full extension.',
  'Cable Rope Hammer Curl': 'Attach a rope to a low cable and hold an end in each hand with a neutral grip, thumbs up. Curl upward keeping your elbows pinned to your sides and maintaining the neutral grip at the top. The cable keeps tension on the brachialis and forearms at the bottom, so lower with control to a full stretch.',
  'Bayesian Curl': 'Set a single handle on a low cable, face away from the pulley, and let your working arm extend back behind your body. Curl the handle up while keeping your elbow fixed and slightly behind your torso. This back-set position loads the long head of the bicep in a deep stretch, so control the eccentric and do not let the elbow drift forward.',
  'Waiter Curl': 'Hold a single dumbbell vertically with both hands cupped under the top plate, like carrying a tray. Curl it up towards your chest keeping your elbows tucked, then lower under control. The supinated, palms-up position keeps strong tension on the biceps throughout, so avoid swinging and keep the movement strict.',
  'TRX Curl': 'Grip the TRX handles with a supinated grip and lean back so your body is at an angle, the more horizontal the harder it is. Curl your body up by bending only at the elbows, keeping your upper arms fixed and high. Squeeze the biceps at the top, then lower under control while keeping your body rigid from head to heels.',
  'Dumbbell Skull Crusher': 'Lie flat holding a dumbbell in each hand with your arms vertical over your chest, palms facing each other. Keeping your upper arms still, lower the dumbbells towards the sides of your head by bending your elbows. Extend back up by straightening the elbows, and do not let your upper arms drift backward.',
  'Tricep Pushdown (Rope)': 'Grip a rope on a high cable with your elbows tucked to your sides. Push down and spread the rope apart at full extension to get a wider tricep squeeze. Control the weight on the way back up, getting a good stretch at the top, and do not let the weight yank your elbows upward.',
  'Tricep Pushdown (Bar)': 'Grip a straight or angled bar on a high cable with your elbows tucked to your sides. Push the bar down to full arm extension, squeezing the triceps hard at the bottom. Control the return without letting the weight pull your elbows up, keeping your torso upright throughout.',
  'Dumbbell Overhead Tricep Extension': 'Hold a single dumbbell with both hands and extend it overhead. Lower it behind your head by bending your elbows, keeping your upper arms close to your ears. Extend fully to lockout and feel the long head stretch at the bottom, keeping your core braced to avoid arching the lower back.',
  'Single Arm Cable Extension': 'Set a single handle on a high cable and grip it with one hand, elbow tucked to your side. Push down to full arm extension, squeezing the tricep at the bottom, then control the return to a full stretch. Working one arm at a time lets you focus on each side evenly, so keep your torso still.',
  'Board Press': 'Set up as you would for a bench press with one or more boards placed on your chest. Lower the bar to the boards, pause briefly, then press back up to lockout. The boards shorten the range and overload the top portion of the press, heavily working the triceps, so keep your shoulder blades retracted throughout.',
  'Close-Grip Floor Press': 'Lie on the floor with a roughly shoulder-width grip on the bar, narrower than a regular bench. Lower the bar until your upper arms rest on the floor, pause briefly, then press straight up. The floor limits the range and protects the shoulders while loading the triceps, so keep your elbows tucked close to your sides.',
  'Reverse Grip Cable Pushdown': 'Grip a straight bar on a high cable with a supinated (underhand) grip, elbows tucked to your sides. Push the bar down to full arm extension, squeezing the triceps at the bottom. The reverse grip emphasises the medial head, so keep your wrists firm and control the return without letting your elbows rise.',
  'Tricep Dip (Parallel Bars)': 'Grip the parallel bars and support yourself with your arms straight, torso upright and elbows close to your body. Lower until your upper arms are roughly parallel to the floor, then drive back up to lockout. Keeping the torso upright targets the triceps, so avoid leaning forward which shifts the load to the chest.',
  'Cable Overhead Tricep Extension': 'Face away from a high cable holding a rope or bar overhead with your elbows bent. Extend your arms fully forward and up, feeling a deep stretch in the long head of the tricep at the bottom. Keep your upper arms close to your ears and your core braced to avoid arching the lower back.',
  'Barbell Lunge': 'Place a barbell across your upper traps and step forward into a lunge, lowering your back knee towards the floor with your front shin relatively vertical. Push through your front heel to return to standing. Keep your torso upright and your core braced, and do not let your front knee cave inward.',
  'Step-Up (Barbell)': 'Place a barbell across your upper traps and step onto a box or bench with one foot, driving through your heel to lift your body up. Bring the other foot up to meet it, then step back down under control. The working leg does all the lifting, so avoid pushing off the back foot for assistance.',
  'Cyclist Squat': 'Elevate your heels on a wedge or small plates and take a roughly shoulder-width stance. Squat down with an upright torso, letting your knees travel forward over your toes to bias the quads. Drive up through the whole foot and squeeze the quads at the top, keeping your chest tall throughout.',
  'Box Squat': 'Set a box behind you at a height that puts you at or just below parallel. Squat back and down with control until you sit lightly on the box without fully relaxing, then drive up explosively. The box reinforces depth and a strong hip hinge, so keep your shins relatively vertical and your spine neutral.',
  'Pause Squat': 'Set up and descend as you would for a back squat, then hold a full pause of 2 to 3 seconds at the bottom while staying tight. Drive up explosively out of the hole without bouncing. The pause removes the stretch reflex and builds strength out of the bottom, so keep your chest tall and your core braced throughout.',
  'Heel-Elevated Squat': 'Place your heels on a small wedge or plates and squat with a fairly upright torso. The raised heels let your knees travel further forward, biasing the quads and allowing a deeper, more comfortable bottom position. Keep your chest tall, push your knees out over your toes, and drive up through your whole foot.',
  'Single Leg Press': 'Place one foot in the centre of the platform and keep your hips square on the seat. Lower the sled until your knee reaches around 90° or slightly more, then press back up without locking out. Working one leg at a time evens out side-to-side imbalances, so control the descent and avoid letting your knee cave inward.',
  'Spanish Squat': 'Loop a band around a sturdy post and the back of your knees, then sit back against it with your shins kept vertical. Squat down keeping your torso upright, letting the band take the strain rather than your knees travelling forward. This isolates the quads with very little load on the joints, so squeeze hard at the bottom and rise under control.',
  'Safety Bar Squat': 'Settle the padded yoke on your traps with the handles in front, which keeps your torso more upright than a straight bar. Brace your core and squat to at least parallel, pushing your knees out to track your toes. The forward weight bias demands an upright chest, so resist any tendency to fold forward and drive up through your whole foot.',
  'Front Squat (Dumbbell)': 'Hold a single dumbbell against your chest in a goblet position or two dumbbells racked on your shoulders. Keep your elbows high and your torso upright as you squat to parallel or below. The front load forces a tall back, so brace your core, push your knees out over your toes, and drive straight up through your heels.',
  'Split Squat': 'Stand in a long staggered stance with your weight mostly on the front foot and the rear heel lifted. Lower straight down until your rear knee nearly touches the floor, keeping your front shin fairly vertical. Drive up through your front heel and avoid pushing off the back foot, keeping your torso tall throughout.',
  'Reverse Lunge': 'Step one foot back and lower your rear knee towards the floor, keeping your front shin relatively vertical. Drive through your front heel to step back to standing. Stepping backward rather than forward is gentler on the front knee and keeps more tension on the working leg, so keep your torso upright and move with control.',
  'Curtsy Lunge': 'Step one foot diagonally behind and across the other, lowering until your rear knee approaches the floor. Keep your hips square and your front knee tracking over your toes rather than caving in. The crossover angle emphasises the glute medius and outer hip, so drive back up through the front heel under control.',
  'Leg Press (High Foot)': 'Set your feet high on the platform, around shoulder width, to shift emphasis onto the glutes and hamstrings. Lower the sled until your knees reach about 90°, then press back up without locking out. The high placement increases hip flexion, so keep your lower back flat against the pad and avoid letting your tailbone curl off the seat.',
  'Cycling (Stationary)': 'Set the saddle so your knee has a slight bend at the bottom of the pedal stroke and sit tall with a relaxed grip on the bars. Pedal in smooth circles, driving down and pulling through rather than mashing the pedals. Adjust resistance to suit your effort target and keep your knees tracking straight rather than splaying out.',
  'Terminal Knee Extension': 'Loop a band around a sturdy post and the back of one knee, then stand far enough away to create tension. Start with a slightly bent knee and straighten it fully against the band, squeezing the quad just above the kneecap. This strengthens the final few degrees of extension, so keep the movement small and controlled rather than swinging the whole leg.',
  'Romanian Deadlift': 'Hold the bar at hip height with a shoulder-width grip and a soft bend in your knees. Push your hips back and lower the bar along your legs until you feel a deep stretch in your hamstrings, keeping your back flat. Drive your hips forward to return to standing, keeping the bar close to your body the whole way.',
  'Nordic Curl': 'Kneel with your feet anchored and your body upright, arms crossed on your chest. Lower your torso towards the floor as slowly as you can, resisting with your hamstrings for as long as possible. Catch yourself with your hands near the bottom and push back up, treating this as one of the hardest hamstring movements there is. Build the range gradually.',
  'Leg Curl (Cable)': 'Attach an ankle strap to a low cable and lie or stand so the cable pulls your foot away from your glute. Curl your heel towards your glute against the resistance, squeezing the hamstring at the top. The cable keeps tension on the muscle throughout, so lower slowly and fully extend the knee between reps.',
  'Glute Ham Raise': 'Set up in the GHR pad with your feet anchored and thighs supported. Lower your torso forward by straightening your knees under hamstring control, then curl yourself back up by driving your heels into the platform. Keep your hips extended and your body in a straight line, treating the descent as slowly and controlled as you can manage.',
  'Single-Leg Romanian Deadlift (DB)': 'Hold a dumbbell and hinge forward on one leg while the other extends straight behind you for balance. Keep your hips square to the floor and your back flat as you lower until you feel a hamstring stretch. Drive through the standing heel to return, using a wall or support until your balance is reliable.',
  'B-Stance Romanian Deadlift': 'Set one foot slightly behind as a kickstand with most of the weight on the front leg. Hinge at the hips and lower the weight along the front leg, feeling the stretch in that hamstring. The kickstand gives just enough balance to load one side hard while keeping the movement stable, so drive the front hip forward to stand.',
  'Deadlift (Conventional)': 'Set the bar over your mid-foot with your shins close, hips high, and back neutral. Take the slack out of the bar, then drive through the floor with your legs while keeping the bar tight to your body. Lock out by driving your hips forward and do not hyperextend your lower back at the top.',
  'Deadlift (Sumo)': 'Take a wide stance with your toes turned out and grip the bar inside your legs. Drop your hips, push the floor away, and drive your knees out to match your toe angle as you lift. Keep the bar close to your body and your chest up throughout, finishing with your hips fully extended.',
  'Trap Bar Deadlift (Hamstring)': 'Stand in the centre of the trap bar and push your hips back to load the hamstrings, keeping your shins more vertical than a quad-biased pull. Grip the handles with a neutral spine, then drive the floor away and stand tall. Emphasising the hip hinge over the knee bend shifts the work onto the hamstrings and glutes, so feel the stretch as you lower.',
  'Prone Leg Curl': 'Lie face down with the pad against the back of your lower calves and your hips pressed into the bench. Curl your heels towards your glutes in a smooth arc, squeezing hard at the top. Lower slowly under control and keep your hips down so the hamstrings do the work rather than your lower back.',
  'Dumbbell Single-Leg RDL': 'Hold a dumbbell and hinge forward on one leg, letting the free leg extend behind you for counterbalance. Keep your hips square and your spine neutral as you lower until you feel the hamstring lengthen. Drive through the standing heel to return, controlling the wobble rather than rushing the rep.',
  'Machine Hip Thrust': 'Sit in the machine with the pad across your hips and your feet planted firmly on the platform. Drive your hips forward by squeezing your glutes until your thighs are roughly parallel, then return under control. The fixed path lets you focus purely on glute force, so pause and squeeze at the top of each rep.',
  'Single Leg Hip Thrust': 'Rest your upper back on a bench, plant one foot, and lift the other leg off the floor. Drive through the planted heel to raise your hips until your thigh is parallel, squeezing the glute hard at the top. Keep your hips level rather than letting them tilt, and lower with control.',
  'Frog Pump': 'Lie on your back, press the soles of your feet together, and let your knees fall open. Drive your hips up by squeezing your glutes, holding briefly at the top before lowering. The frog position emphasises the glutes through outward hip rotation, so focus on the squeeze rather than the height of the lift.',
  'Donkey Kick': 'Start on all fours with your hands under your shoulders and knees under your hips. Keeping your knee bent, drive one heel towards the ceiling by squeezing the glute, stopping when your thigh is in line with your torso. Avoid arching your lower back to gain height, and lower under control.',
  'Abduction Machine': 'Sit with the pads against the outsides of your knees and your back against the seat. Push your knees outward against the resistance, squeezing the glutes and outer hips at the fully open position. Avoid using momentum, returning slowly under control rather than letting the stack pull your knees back in.',
  'Step-Up (Glute Focus)': 'Use a box high enough to create a deep hip bend and step up driving through the heel of the working leg. Lean your torso slightly forward to bias the glute, and bring the trailing foot up without pushing off it. Step back down under control, letting the working leg do all the lifting.',
  'Sumo Squat (Glute Focus)': 'Take a wide stance with your toes pointing out and hold a dumbbell or kettlebell between your legs. Squat down while pushing your knees out hard to match your toes, then drive up by squeezing the glutes. The wide stance loads the inner thighs and glutes, so keep your chest tall and back flat throughout.',
  'Nordic Glute Curl': 'Anchor your feet and kneel tall with your hips slightly flexed forward to bias the glutes and hamstrings together. Lower your torso under control, resisting with the back of your legs, then pull yourself back up. Keeping a slight forward hip lean shifts more emphasis onto the glute tie-in, so build the range gradually.',
  'Reverse Hyperextension (Glute)': 'Lie face down with your hips at the edge of the machine and hold the handles. Raise your legs behind you by squeezing your glutes, keeping a slight knee bend and stopping in line with your torso. Avoid swinging or over-arching the lower back, lowering under control to feel the stretch before each rep.',
  'Sumo Deadlift (Glute Focus)': 'Take a wide stance with toes turned out and grip the bar inside your legs. Drop your hips and drive through the floor, pushing your knees out and squeezing your glutes hard at lockout. The wide stance and upright torso load the glutes strongly, so finish each rep with a deliberate hip squeeze rather than leaning back.',
  'Sumo Deadlift (Wide Stance)': 'Set your feet very wide with toes turned well out and grip the bar inside your legs. The wide stance shortens the bar travel and demands strong hip drive, so push your knees out to match your toes as you stand. Keep the bar close to your body and your chest up, finishing with full hip extension.',
  'Cable Donkey Kickback': 'Attach an ankle strap to a low cable and get on all fours facing away from the pulley. Drive one leg back and up by squeezing the glute, keeping the knee bent or straight depending on preference. The cable keeps tension throughout, so avoid arching your lower back and control the return.',
  'Donkey Kickback (Machine)': 'Position yourself in the machine with the pad against the sole or back of one foot. Drive the foot back and up by squeezing the glute, stopping in line with your torso. Keep your hips square and your lower back neutral rather than arching to gain range, and lower under control.',
  'Walking Lunge (Glute Focus)': 'Take a longer stride than usual and lean your torso slightly forward to bias the glutes. Lower your back knee towards the floor, then drive off the front heel to step straight into the next lunge. The longer step increases the stretch on the glute, so keep your torso steady and avoid pushing off the trailing toes.',
  'Curtsy Lunge (Glute Focus)': 'Step one foot diagonally behind and across the other, lowering under control with your weight on the front heel. Keep your hips square and let the front glute and outer hip do the work as you drive back up. The crossover angle targets the glute medius, so resist letting the front knee cave inward.',
  'Kickstand Hip Thrust': 'Rest your upper back on a bench and plant one foot firmly while the other rests lightly as a kickstand for balance. Drive through the working heel to raise your hips until your thigh is parallel, squeezing that glute hard. The kickstand lets you load one side nearly as hard as a single-leg version while staying stable.',
  'Cable Pull-Through (Glute)': 'Face away from a low cable with the rope drawn between your legs and the cable behind you. Hinge at the hips with a neutral spine, then drive your hips forward to stand, squeezing your glutes at the top. This trains the hip hinge with glute emphasis, so let your hips do the work rather than pulling with your arms.',
  'Romanian Deadlift (Glute)': 'Hold the bar at hip height and push your hips back with a soft knee bend, lowering the bar along your legs. Emphasise driving the hips back and forward rather than chasing depth, squeezing the glutes hard as you stand tall. Keep the bar close to your body and stop lowering once the hamstring stretch peaks.',
  'Standing Calf Raise (Barbell)': 'Place a barbell across your traps and stand with the balls of your feet on a raised block or plate. Lower your heels for a full stretch, then rise as high as possible onto your toes, holding the top briefly. The barbell load lets you push the calves hard, so keep your knees nearly straight and the tempo slow.',
  'Seated Machine Calf Raise': 'Sit with the pads across your lower thighs just above the knees and the balls of your feet on the platform. Lower your heels for a deep stretch, then press up to full plantarflexion and hold the squeeze. The bent-knee position biases the soleus, so use a full range and a slow, controlled tempo.',
  'Seated Dumbbell Calf Raise': 'Sit on a bench and rest dumbbells across your lower thighs, with the balls of your feet on a block or plate. Lower your heels for a full stretch, then drive your toes down to raise the heels as high as you can. The seated position targets the soleus, so hold the squeeze at the top and lower slowly.',
  'Calf Raise on Steps': 'Stand with the balls of your feet on the edge of a step and your heels hanging off, holding a support for balance. Sink your heels low for a deep stretch, then rise fully onto your toes and hold briefly. The step provides the range a flat floor cannot, so move slowly and avoid bouncing out of the bottom.',
  'Single-Leg Calf Raise (Dumbbell)': 'Hold a dumbbell in one hand and stand on one foot on the edge of a step, using your free hand for balance. Sink the heel low for a full stretch, then rise onto the tip of your big toe and hold the squeeze. Working one leg at a time removes any assistance, so keep the tempo slow and the range full.',
  'Calf Raise on Leg Press Sled': 'Place just the balls of your feet on the bottom edge of the leg press platform with a slight bend kept in your knees. Let the sled push your toes back for a deep stretch, then press to full plantarflexion. Do not lock your knees, and keep the movement controlled through a full range at the bottom and top.',
  'Standing Calf Raise (Bodyweight)': 'Stand tall, ideally with the balls of your feet on a small raise, and use a wall for balance if needed. Lower your heels for a stretch, then rise as high as possible onto your toes and squeeze. With only bodyweight, slow the tempo and add reps or single-leg work to keep the calves challenged.',
  'Tibialis Raise (Wall)': 'Stand with your back and hips against a wall and your feet a step or so out in front. Keeping your heels down, pull your toes up towards your shins as high as you can, then lower slowly. This strengthens the shin muscles to balance the calves, so control the lowering rather than dropping the toes.',
  'Tibialis Raise (Slant Board)': 'Stand on a slant board facing downhill with your heels secured or weighted. Pull your toes up towards your shins against the incline, squeezing the front of the shin, then lower under control. The board increases the range and load on the tibialis, so keep the movement slow and deliberate.',
  'Toe Walk': 'Rise onto the balls of your feet and walk forward staying high on your toes throughout. Keep your ankles steady and your posture tall rather than letting your heels touch down. This builds calf endurance and ankle stability, so cover a set distance or time while maintaining the raised position.',
  'Ab Wheel Rollout': 'Kneel with your hands on the wheel and your core braced hard. Roll forward slowly, extending your body as far as you can without your lower back sagging, then contract your abs to pull yourself back in. This is very demanding, so limit your range at first and never let your hips drop into an arch.',
  'Landmine Rotation': 'Hold the end of a barbell with both hands extended in front of your chest, standing in an athletic stance. Rotate the bar from side to side in a controlled arc, turning through your thoracic spine while your hips stay relatively stable. Control the movement rather than throwing the bar, and keep your arms long throughout.',
  'Crunch': 'Lie on your back with your knees bent and feet flat on the floor. Curl your ribcage towards your pelvis, lifting your shoulder blades off the ground rather than yanking your head with your hands. Hold the contraction briefly at the top, then lower under control. The movement is short, so focus on flexing the abs, not sitting all the way up.',
  'Reverse Crunch': 'Lie on your back with your hands by your sides or holding a support overhead. Draw your knees towards your chest and curl your hips off the floor, lifting your pelvis towards your ribcage. Lower slowly with control rather than letting your legs drop, keeping the movement driven by the lower abs.',
  'Leg Raise': 'Lie flat on your back with your hands tucked under your hips or by your sides. Keeping your legs straight or slightly bent, raise them towards the ceiling, then lower them under control without letting your lower back arch off the floor. Stop lowering before your back lifts, and keep the abs engaged throughout.',
  'Machine Crunch': 'Sit in the machine with the pad against your chest or shoulders and grip the handles. Flex your spine to crunch your ribcage towards your pelvis against the resistance, squeezing your abs at the bottom. Return under control rather than letting the stack pull you upright, keeping the motion in your trunk rather than your arms.',
  'Dead Bug': 'Lie on your back with your arms reaching towards the ceiling and your knees bent over your hips. Slowly extend one arm overhead and the opposite leg towards the floor, keeping your lower back pressed flat. Return and alternate sides, moving slowly so the abs stay braced and the back never arches.',
  'Hollow Body Hold': 'Lie on your back, press your lower back into the floor, and lift your shoulders and legs a few inches off the ground. Reach your arms overhead and hold this dished position with your abs braced hard. Keep your lower back glued to the floor throughout, lowering your limbs closer to the ground only if you can maintain that contact.',
  'Stir the Pot': 'Set your forearms on a stability ball in a plank position with your body in a straight line. Brace your core and make small controlled circles with the ball, as if stirring a pot, in both directions. The aim is to keep your torso rigid while the arms move, so resist any sagging or rotation through the hips.',
  'Hanging Knee Raise': 'Hang from a bar with your arms fully extended and shoulders engaged. Raise your knees towards your chest by curling your pelvis upward, not just bending at the hips. Lower with control and avoid swinging, keeping the movement driven by the abs rather than momentum.',
  'Cable Woodchop (High to Low)': 'Set the cable high and grasp the handle with both hands, standing side-on to the pulley. Pull and rotate the handle down and across your body towards the opposite hip, turning through your thoracic spine. Keep your hips relatively stable and control the return rather than letting the cable pull you back up.',
  'Cable Woodchop (Low to High)': 'Set the cable low and grasp the handle with both hands, standing side-on to the pulley. Pull and rotate the handle up and across your body towards the opposite shoulder, driving through your trunk. Keep your hips fairly square and control the return, avoiding any jerking from the lower back.',
  'Ab Wheel (Kneeling)': 'Kneel with both hands on the wheel and brace your abs hard before you move. Roll the wheel forward slowly, extending as far as you can while keeping your lower back from sagging, then contract to pull yourself back. Start with a short range and only extend further as your core strength allows.',
  'Weighted Sit-Up': 'Lie with your knees bent and feet anchored or flat, holding a plate or dumbbell against your chest. Sit up by curling your spine towards your knees, then lower slowly under control. Keep the weight close to your body and avoid pulling on your neck, letting the abs drive the movement.',
  'Toe-to-Bar': 'Hang from a pull-up bar with an engaged shoulder and a tight core. Raise your legs in a controlled arc until your toes touch the bar, curling your pelvis upward rather than swinging. Lower under control and reset between reps, keeping momentum out of the movement.',
  'L-Sit Hold': 'Support yourself on parallettes, dip bars, or the floor with your arms locked out and shoulders pressed down. Lift your legs straight out in front so your body forms an L, holding the position with your abs and hip flexors. Keep your legs as straight as you can and your shoulders depressed, building the hold time gradually.',
  'Suitcase Carry': 'Hold a single heavy dumbbell or kettlebell in one hand and stand tall with your shoulders level. Walk a set distance, bracing your core hard to stop your torso leaning towards the weight. The anti-lateral-flexion challenge is the point, so keep your spine upright and switch sides to balance the load.',
  'Pallof Press (Kneeling)': 'Kneel side-on to a cable set at chest height and hold the handle at your chest with both hands. Press straight out and hold, resisting the cable\'s pull to twist you towards the pulley. This is an anti-rotation hold, so keep your hips and torso square and still throughout, returning the handle under control.',
  'Copenhagen Plank': 'Lie on your side and rest your top foot on a bench, with your bottom leg either hanging or supporting beneath. Lift your hips so your body forms a straight line, driving down through the top leg into the bench. This strongly loads the adductors and obliques, so start with the bottom leg supporting and progress slowly.',
  'Power Clean': 'Set up over the bar as for a deadlift, then explosively extend your hips, knees, and ankles to drive the bar upward. Pull yourself under as the bar rises and catch it on your front shoulders in a quarter squat, elbows high. This is a fast, technical lift, so prioritise a clean triple extension and a solid front-rack catch over heavy load.',
  'Rack Pull (Traps)': 'Set the bar in a rack at around knee height and grip it just outside your legs with a tall chest. Drive through your legs and hips to stand tall, then hold the top and let your traps and upper back take the load. The shortened range lets you handle heavy weight, so keep your spine neutral and avoid leaning back at lockout.',
  'Face Pull (Traps)': 'Set a rope at face height and pull towards your forehead with your elbows flaring high and wide. Drive the contraction up and back so your upper traps and rear delts work together at the top. Keep the load light and the tempo controlled rather than yanking with momentum.',
  'Incline Shrug': 'Lie chest-down on an incline bench set to around 45° and let dumbbells hang straight towards the floor. Shrug your shoulder blades up and slightly back, squeezing the traps at the top. The incline angle shifts emphasis towards the mid and lower traps compared with a standing shrug.',
  'Snatch Grip Shrug': 'Take a very wide, snatch-width grip on the barbell and stand tall with the bar at thigh height. Shrug straight up, elevating the shoulders as high as possible while keeping the arms straight. The wide grip increases the range and brings the upper back into the movement more.',
  'Keg Carry': 'Hug the keg tight against your chest with both arms and brace your core hard before lifting. Walk with short, deliberate steps and an upright torso, fighting the shifting liquid inside. Keep your chest tall and do not let the load pull you into a rounded back.',
  'Thick Bar Curl': 'Grip the thick bar with a supinated grip and keep your elbows pinned to your sides. Curl smoothly to the top and squeeze the biceps, then lower under control. The increased bar diameter forces the forearms and grip to work much harder than a standard bar.',
  'Cable Reverse Curl (Forearms)': 'Attach a straight bar to a low cable and grip it overhand at shoulder width. Curl the bar up with your elbows pinned, leading with the back of the hands. The pronated grip targets the brachioradialis and wrist extensors while the cable keeps tension at the bottom.',
  'Farmer Walk (Forearms)': 'Grip heavy dumbbells or handles at your sides and stand tall with shoulders pulled back. Walk for distance or time, gripping the load as hard as you can throughout. The constant heavy hold is what drives forearm and grip development, so resist the urge to set down early.',
  'Towel Pull-Up': 'Drape one or two towels over a pull-up bar and grip them firmly, one hand per towel. Pull yourself up until your chin clears your hands, keeping your core tight to limit swing. The thick, unstable towel grip dramatically increases the demand on your forearms and fingers.',
  'Fat Grip Curl': 'Fit fat grips around the dumbbell or bar handle and curl with your usual supinated technique. Keep your elbows fixed at your sides and squeeze at the top. The enlarged handle forces a far harder grip, building the forearms alongside the biceps.',
  'Hand Gripper': 'Hold the gripper in your palm with the handle running across the base of your fingers, not deep in the palm. Squeeze the handles fully closed, hold the contraction briefly, then release slowly. Controlling the opening phase trains the grip just as much as the close.',
  'Rice Bucket': 'Plunge your hand into a deep bucket of rice and perform movements such as squeezing, spreading the fingers, and rotating the wrist. Work against the resistance of the rice through a full range for each movement. This builds endurance and strengthens the smaller muscles of the hand and forearm.',
  'Pinch Grip Carry': 'Pinch a plate or block between your fingers and thumb with the smooth side out, gripping as hard as you can. Stand tall and walk for distance or time without letting the load slip. This directly targets the thumb and finger flexors that drive pinch strength.',
  'Cable Wrist Curl': 'Kneel or sit and rest your forearms on a bench with your palms facing up towards a low cable. Curl the cable upward using only your wrists, then lower fully for a stretch. The cable maintains tension at the bottom where a free weight would lose it.',
  'Cable Reverse Wrist Curl': 'Rest your forearms on a bench with your palms facing down towards a low cable. Extend your wrists upward against the cable, working the extensors on the top of the forearm. Use light resistance as these muscles are far weaker than the flexors.',
  'Neck Flexion (Machine)': 'Set the pad against your forehead and adjust the seat so the resistance lines up with the movement. Flex your neck forward smoothly, bringing your chin towards your chest, then return slowly. Use a light load and a controlled tempo. The neck never responds well to heavy, jerky reps.',
  'Neck Extension (Machine)': 'Position the pad against the back of your head and sit so the resistance tracks the movement. Extend your neck backward in a smooth arc, then return under control to the start. Keep the load light and the motion deliberate to protect the cervical spine.',
  'Neck Lateral Flexion': 'Set or hold the resistance against the side of your head and keep your shoulders square and level. Bend your neck sideways, bringing your ear towards your shoulder, then return slowly. Move only at the neck and avoid hiking the shoulder up to meet the head.',
  'Neck Curl': 'Lie on your back on a bench with your head hanging off the end and a light plate held on your forehead. Curl your chin towards your chest through a full range, then lower slowly. Keep the load light and never use momentum on the neck.',
  'Neck Bridge': 'Lie on your back and carefully raise your hips, taking weight onto the back of your head and your feet. Rock gently forward and back through a small, controlled range. This is an advanced movement. Build neck strength with lighter exercises first and never load it aggressively.',
  'Plate Neck Curl': 'Lie face up on a bench with your head off the end and hold a light plate on your forehead with a towel for comfort. Curl your chin towards your chest, then lower slowly under control. Keep the weight modest and the tempo smooth throughout.',
  'Plate Neck Extension': 'Lie face down on a bench with your head off the end and hold a light plate against the back of your head with a towel. Extend your neck upward, then lower slowly to the start. Use a light load and avoid any sudden or jerky movement.',
  'Neck Harness Flexion': 'Fit the harness over your head and attach a light load to the chain hanging in front. Flex your neck forward against the resistance, bringing your chin towards your chest, then return slowly. Keep the weight conservative and the motion controlled at all times.',
  'Neck Harness Extension': 'Fit the harness and let the loaded chain hang behind you, sitting with a slight forward lean. Extend your neck backward against the resistance, then return under control. Use light weight and a smooth tempo to protect the cervical spine.',
  'Manual Resistance Neck Flexion': 'Place your own palm against your forehead to provide resistance and brace your torso. Flex your neck forward into your hand, matching the resistance to a level you can control smoothly. Move slowly through the range and never overpower the neck with force.',
  'Manual Resistance Neck Extension': 'Cup your palm against the back of your head to provide resistance. Extend your neck backward into your hand, applying only as much pressure as you can control smoothly. Keep the movement slow and deliberate throughout.',
  'Neck Rotation (Resistance)': 'Apply resistance with your hand or a band against the side of your head and keep your shoulders square. Rotate your head to look over your shoulder against the resistance, then return slowly. Use very light resistance and a controlled tempo to protect the neck.',
  'Sled Push': 'Grip the uprights, drop into an athletic lean with arms extended, and brace your core. Drive the sled forward with powerful, alternating leg strides, keeping a straight line from head to heels. Maintain a low, forward body angle and push through the full leg drive on each step.',
  'Sled Pull': 'Attach a strap or rope and walk backward or hinge to pull the sled towards you. Lean away from the load, brace your core, and drive through your legs with each step. Keep the line of pull tight and your steps controlled rather than rushed.',
  'Prowler Drag': 'Attach a strap to the prowler and walk backward, leaning back against the load with arms extended. Drive through your quads with each step, staying low and controlled. The backward drag heavily loads the quads and is easy on the joints.',
  'Battle Ropes': 'Stand in a quarter-squat with a rope end in each hand and your core braced. Drive the ropes in fast, powerful waves, alternating or moving both arms together. Keep your knees soft and your torso stable. The power should come from the whole body, not just the arms.',
  'Assault Bike': 'Sit tall, grip the handles, and drive with both your legs and arms in unison. Keep your core engaged and pull as well as push on the handles for full-body output. Set a steady cadence for endurance or alternate hard bursts with recovery for intervals.',
  'Tyre Flip': 'Set up in a deep squat with your chest against the tyre and your hands underneath the bottom edge. Drive explosively through your legs and hips to lift it to vertical, then push it over with your chest and arms. Lift with your legs, not your back, and keep your spine neutral throughout.',
  'Kettlebell Snatch': 'Hike the kettlebell back between your legs, then drive your hips forward explosively to send it overhead in one motion. Punch your hand around the bell at the top to avoid it banging the wrist, finishing with a locked-out arm. The power comes from the hip snap, not the arm.',
  'Kettlebell Clean and Press': 'Hike the bell back, then drive your hips to clean it to the rack position against your forearm, taming the flip so it lands softly. From the rack, brace your core and press the bell overhead to lockout. Lower back to the rack and then to the start under control.',
  'Jump Squat': 'Drop into a quarter to half squat, then explode upward into a jump, fully extending your hips, knees, and ankles. Land softly on the balls of your feet and absorb the impact by bending the knees. Reset your posture between reps rather than rushing into the next jump.',
  'Broad Jump': 'Stand with feet hip-width, hinge into a quarter squat, and swing your arms back. Drive forward and up explosively, jumping for maximum horizontal distance, then land softly with bent knees. Stick the landing in a balanced position before resetting for the next rep.',
  'Depth Jump': 'Step off a low box and land softly on both feet, then immediately rebound into a maximal vertical jump. Minimise your ground contact time. The aim is to spend as little time on the floor as possible. Start with a low box height and progress carefully as this is high impact.',
  'Stair Running': 'Run up the stairs driving through the balls of your feet, pumping your arms and keeping your torso slightly forward. Take one or two steps at a time depending on your stride. Walk back down to recover. This is a powerful conditioning and lower-body drill.',
  'Clean Pull': 'Set up over the bar as for a clean, with hips down and back flat. Drive through the floor and accelerate the bar upward with a powerful hip extension, shrugging hard at the top. The arms stay straight throughout. This trains the explosive pull without the catch.',
  'Barbell Good Morning': 'Place the bar on your upper traps and keep a slight bend in your knees. Hinge at the hips, pushing them back and lowering your torso towards horizontal while keeping a neutral spine. Drive your hips forward to return upright. Prioritise back position over how far you lower.',
  'Zercher Squat': 'Cradle the bar in the crooks of your elbows and hold it tight against your torso with your core braced. Squat down with an upright posture, keeping your elbows up and chest tall. Drive up through your whole foot. The front-loaded position demands a very upright back.',
  'Anderson Squat': 'Set the safety pins so the bar rests at or below parallel inside the rack. Begin each rep from a dead stop at the bottom, driving up explosively with no stretch reflex to help. This builds raw strength out of the hole. Reset fully on the pins between reps.',
  'Pin Squat': 'Set the safety pins at your target depth and squat down to rest the bar lightly on them. Pause briefly to remove momentum, then drive up powerfully. The dead-stop pause builds strength and reinforces a strong position at a specific point in the squat.',
  'Hatfield Squat': 'Hold onto a fixed support or the rack uprights for balance while the bar sits on a safety squat bar across your back. Use the support only to stay upright, not to lift, as you squat deep and drive back up. This lets you push the legs hard with reduced balance demand.',
  'Cambered Bar Squat': 'Set the cambered bar across your traps and brace hard. The hanging weight will sway, so control the bar throughout the squat by keeping a tight upper back and a braced core. Squat to depth and drive up while fighting to keep the load stable.',
  'SSB Squat': 'Settle the safety squat bar on your traps with the handles in front and grip them lightly. The forward cam shifts the load slightly forward, so stay tall and resist the tendency to fold forward. Squat to depth and drive up through your whole foot, keeping your chest up.',
  'Jefferson Curl': 'Stand on a box holding a light weight and slowly roll down one vertebra at a time, letting the spine flex fully as you reach towards your feet. Keep your legs nearly straight and the weight light. Reverse the motion to roll back up. This is a loaded stretch. Start very light and progress slowly.',
  'Stiff-Leg Deadlift (Dumbbell)': 'Hold dumbbells in front of your thighs and keep your legs nearly straight with only a soft knee bend. Hinge at the hips, lowering the dumbbells along your legs until you feel a deep hamstring stretch. Drive your hips forward to stand. Keep your back flat throughout. Do not round to gain range.',
  'Serratus Punch': 'Lie on your back or stand holding a light weight with your arm extended upward towards the ceiling. Punch the weight further up by protracting your shoulder blade, then lower under control. The small range of scapular movement is the focus, isolating the serratus anterior.',
  'Kneeling Cable Crunch': 'Kneel facing a high cable with a rope held by your head and your hips set back. Flex your spine, crunching your ribcage towards your pelvis rather than just pulling with the arms. Keep your hips fixed and squeeze the abs hard at the bottom of each rep.',
  'Exercise Ball Crunch': 'Sit on a stability ball and walk your feet out so your lower back is supported on the ball. Crunch your ribcage towards your pelvis, then lower back over the ball to stretch the abs. The ball allows a greater range of motion than a floor crunch.',
  'Mountain Climber': 'Start in a high plank with hands under your shoulders and your body in a straight line. Drive one knee towards your chest, then quickly switch legs in a running motion. Keep your hips low and your core braced. Do not let your hips bounce up and down.',
  'Plank Row': 'Set up in a high plank gripping a dumbbell in each hand, feet a little wider than usual for stability. Row one dumbbell to your hip while keeping your hips square and level to the floor. The main challenge is resisting rotation, so move slowly and control the anti-rotation.',
  'Bear Crawl': 'Start on hands and knees, then lift your knees just off the floor so your weight is on your hands and toes. Crawl forward by moving opposite hand and foot together, keeping your hips low and your back flat. Move slowly and keep your core braced to limit any hip sway.',
  'Kneeling Ab Rollout': 'Kneel with your hands on the wheel and your core braced hard. Roll forward slowly, extending your body as far as you can without letting your lower back sag. Contract your abs to pull yourself back in. Start with a short range and extend it only as your strength allows.',
  'Dumbbell Side Bend': 'Stand holding a dumbbell in one hand with that arm at your side. Bend sideways towards the weighted side, then contract your opposite oblique to pull yourself back upright. Keep the movement in the frontal plane and avoid leaning forward or back. Work both sides evenly.',
  'Cable Side Bend': 'Stand side-on to a low cable and hold the handle at your side. Bend away from the machine against the cable resistance, then return under control by contracting the obliques. The cable keeps tension throughout the range. Keep the motion strictly sideways.',
  'Windmill': 'Hold a weight overhead in a locked-out arm with your feet angled and stance wide. Push your hip out towards the weighted side and hinge down, sliding your free hand down your front leg while keeping your eyes on the overhead weight. Reverse to stand. Keep the top arm locked throughout.',
  'Turkish Get-Up': 'Start lying down with a weight pressed straight up in one hand and your eyes on it. Move through the sequence of roll to elbow, to hand, bridge the hips, sweep the leg through to a lunge, and stand, keeping the weight locked overhead throughout. Reverse the steps to return. Move slowly and master each stage before loading heavily.',
  'Close-Grip Push-Up': 'Place your hands close together, roughly shoulder-width or narrower, under your chest. Lower your chest towards your hands keeping your elbows tucked close to your body. Press back to full extension. The narrow hand position shifts the emphasis onto the triceps.',
  'Archer Push-Up': 'Set up wide in a push-up position and lower towards one hand, keeping that elbow bent while the opposite arm stays nearly straight. Press back up through the working arm, then alternate to the other side. This builds towards the one-arm push-up by loading one side at a time.',
  'Single-Arm Push-Up': 'Set your feet wide for a stable base and place one hand under your chest with the other behind your back. Lower under control, resisting the strong urge to rotate, then press back up. Keep your hips and shoulders as square to the floor as possible. This is highly advanced.',
  'Cable Chest Press (Standing)': 'Set the cables at chest height and stand in a staggered stance facing away from the pulleys, a handle in each hand. Press forward and slightly inward until your hands nearly meet, then return under control. Brace your core to stay stable against the backward pull.',
  'Smith Machine Incline Press': 'Set an incline bench at 30 to 45° under the Smith bar so the bar tracks over your upper chest. Lower the bar to your upper chest with elbows at around 60°, then press up and slightly back. The fixed path removes the need to balance, so focus on the upper chest.',
  'Guillotine Press': 'Lie flat and grip the bar wide, lowering it slowly towards your upper neck or collarbone rather than the lower chest. Keep the descent strictly controlled and stop well short of any discomfort. This places a strong stretch on the upper chest but is risky. Use light weight and a spotter.',
  'Barbell Upright Row (Wide)': 'Grip the bar wider than shoulder width and pull it upward, leading with the elbows. Raise until your elbows reach about shoulder height, then lower under control. The wide grip reduces the internal rotation and lowers impingement risk compared with a narrow grip.',
  'Cable Face Pull (Upper Back)': 'Set a rope at face height and pull towards your forehead with your elbows driving high and out wide. Focus on squeezing the shoulder blades together and back at the end position to load the upper back. Keep the weight light and the tempo controlled.',
  'Smith Machine Row': 'Set the Smith bar low and hinge to a near-horizontal torso with a flat back. Pull the bar to your lower ribs, leading with the elbows and squeezing the shoulder blades together. The fixed path lets you focus purely on the back. Lower to a full stretch each rep.',
  'Half-Kneeling Cable Row': 'Kneel on one knee facing a cable and brace your core to keep your torso tall and stable. Row the handle to your lower ribs, driving your elbow back and squeezing the lat. The half-kneeling stance challenges your core stability while you train the back.',
  'V-Bar Pulldown': 'Attach a V-bar to the pulldown and sit with your thighs secured, leaning back slightly. Pull the bar to your upper chest, driving your elbows down and back, then control the bar back up to a full stretch. The neutral close grip allows a long range and a strong lat contraction.',
  'Wide-Grip Cable Row': 'Use a wide bar on the seated cable row and pull it towards your upper abdomen with a wide overhand grip. Squeeze the shoulder blades together at the contraction. The wide, high pull angle shifts emphasis towards the upper back and rear delts. Let the arms stretch forward between reps.',
  'Barbell Front Raise': 'Hold a barbell in front of your thighs with an overhand shoulder-width grip. Raise the bar forward and upward in a controlled arc until it reaches shoulder height, keeping a slight bend in your elbows. Lower slowly and avoid swinging or using your hips to heave the weight up.',
  'Plate Front Raise': 'Grip a weight plate at the sides or by the edges and hold it in front of your thighs. Raise the plate forward to shoulder height, keeping your arms long with just a slight elbow bend. Lower it slowly under control and resist the urge to lean back or swing.',
  'Kneeling Dumbbell Press': 'Kneel upright on both knees with dumbbells at ear height, palms facing forward. Press straight overhead without arching your lower back, then lower with control. The kneeling position removes leg drive and forces your core and shoulders to do all the work.',
  'Single-Arm Dumbbell Press': 'Press one dumbbell overhead at a time from ear height, palms facing forward. Brace your core hard to resist the tendency to lean towards the working side. The unilateral load exposes side-to-side imbalances and demands extra core stability.',
  'Half-Kneeling Shoulder Press': 'Set up in a half-kneeling position with one knee down and the opposite foot planted in front. Press the weight overhead while keeping your torso upright and your core braced. This stance limits lower back arch and improves pressing stability.',
  'Chin-Up (Supinated)': 'Hang from the bar with a supinated (underhand) grip about shoulder width apart. Pull until your chin clears the bar, keeping your elbows tracking close to your sides. The underhand grip recruits the biceps strongly alongside the lats and allows a powerful squeeze at the top.',
  'Seated Dumbbell Curl': 'Sit on a bench with dumbbells hanging at your sides, palms facing forward. Curl the weights up while keeping your upper arms still and your elbows pinned to your sides. The seated position reduces body sway and keeps tension honest on the biceps.',
  'Lying Cable Curl': 'Lie on the floor or a flat bench facing a low cable, arms extended towards the pulley. Curl the bar or handle towards your forehead while keeping your upper arms stationary. The lying position eliminates cheating and keeps constant tension on the biceps.',
  'High Cable Curl': 'Set the cables at or above shoulder height and grip a handle in each hand with arms out to the sides. Curl your hands towards your head, squeezing the biceps peak hard at the top. This emphasises the short head and gives a strong contraction in the shortened position.',
  'Barbell Drag Curl': 'Curl the barbell while dragging it up along your torso, allowing your elbows to travel backward rather than staying fixed. Keep the bar in contact with your body throughout the rise. This shifts emphasis onto the long head of the biceps and removes front delt involvement.',
  'Smith Machine Close-Grip Press': 'Set the bar over your lower chest and take a grip roughly shoulder width, narrower than a standard bench. Lower the bar with your elbows tucked close to your sides, then press straight up. The fixed bar path lets you focus entirely on loading the triceps.',
  'Dip Machine': 'Sit upright with your back against the pad and grip the handles at your sides. Press down until your arms are nearly straight, squeezing the triceps at the bottom, then return with control. Keep your torso upright to keep tension on the triceps rather than the chest.',
  'Cable Kickback (Triceps)': 'Face a low cable, hinge forward slightly, and hold the handle with your upper arm parallel to the floor. Extend your forearm back until the arm is straight, squeezing the triceps at lockout. Keep your upper arm fixed and avoid swinging the weight.',
  'Barbell Skull Crusher': 'Lie flat holding a barbell with a narrow grip, arms vertical over your chest. Keeping your upper arms still, bend at the elbows to lower the bar towards your forehead or just behind your head. Extend back up by straightening the elbows and do not let the upper arms drift.',
  'Jefferson Squat': 'Straddle the barbell with one foot in front and one behind, gripping it with one hand in front and one behind. Squat down keeping a neutral spine, then drive up through both legs. Alternate stance between sets to balance the asymmetric loading.',
  'Kneeling Squat': 'Kneel upright with a barbell across your upper back or hips and your shins flat on a pad. Hinge back to sit towards your heels, then drive your hips forward to return upright. This loads the glutes heavily while removing the knee and ankle contribution of a standing squat.',
  'Skater Squat': 'Balance on one leg with the other leg bent behind you, holding light weights in front for counterbalance. Lower until your rear knee nearly touches the floor, keeping your front shin fairly vertical. Drive up through the front heel without letting the knee cave inward.',
  'Step-Up (Weighted)': 'Hold dumbbells or a barbell and step onto a box, driving up through the heel of the working leg. Bring the trailing foot up to meet it, then step back down under control. Let the top leg do the lifting and avoid pushing off the bottom foot.',
  'Landmine Squat': 'Hold the end of the barbell at chest height and stand facing the landmine. Squat down keeping your chest tall, letting the bar arc naturally, then drive up through your whole foot. The landmine path supports an upright torso and is gentle on the lower back.',
  'Hip Extension (Cable)': 'Attach an ankle strap to a low cable and face the pulley, holding a support for balance. Kick the working leg straight back, squeezing the glute at full extension. Keep your torso still and avoid arching your lower back to extend further.',
  'Stiff-Leg Deadlift (Single-Leg)': 'Hinge forward on one leg with the other extending behind you, legs kept nearly straight. Keep your hips square and your back flat as you lower until you feel a deep hamstring stretch. Drive through the standing heel to return and use a support until your balance is solid.',
  'Glute Squeeze Hold': 'Set up in a hip thrust or bridge position with your hips fully extended. Squeeze your glutes as hard as possible and hold the contracted position for time. The goal is maximal isometric tension, so focus on the squeeze rather than any movement.',
  'Reverse Lunge (Glute Focus)': 'Step backward into a lunge and lower your back knee towards the floor while keeping a slight forward torso lean. Drive up through the front heel to emphasise the glute. The forward lean and heel drive shift more load onto the glutes than a standard reverse lunge.',
  'Monster Walk': 'Place a resistance band around your ankles or just above your knees and adopt a quarter-squat stance. Step forward and outward in a diagonal pattern, keeping constant tension on the band. Maintain the squat position throughout and keep your knees pushed out against the band.',
  'Agility Ladder Drills': 'Move quickly through the ladder rungs with light, fast footwork, staying on the balls of your feet. Keep your knees soft, your posture upright, and your movements crisp and controlled. Prioritise accurate foot placement and speed over heavy effort.',
  'Rope Jump': 'Hold the handles at hip height and turn the rope with your wrists rather than your whole arms. Jump just high enough to clear the rope, landing softly on the balls of your feet. Keep your jumps small and your rhythm steady to conserve energy.',
  'Heel Walk': 'Lift the balls of your feet off the floor and walk forward on your heels only, toes pointed up. Keep your steps controlled and your posture tall. This strengthens the tibialis anterior at the front of the shin and helps balance lower leg development.',
  'Seated Tibialis Raise': 'Sit with your heels planted and the balls of your feet free, optionally with a weight over your toes. Pull your toes up towards your shins against the resistance, then lower slowly. This directly trains the tibialis anterior at the front of the shin.',
  'Trap Bar Shrug': 'Stand inside the trap bar and grip the handles at your sides with a neutral grip. Shrug straight upward, elevating your shoulders towards your ears, then lower fully. The neutral grip and central loading make this very comfortable for heavy shrugs.',
  'Cable Upright Row (Traps)': 'Set a straight bar or rope on a low cable and grip it at about shoulder width. Pull upward leading with your elbows until they reach roughly shoulder height. The constant cable tension keeps the traps loaded throughout, and a wider grip reduces shoulder impingement risk.',
  'Dumbbell Pronation/Supination': 'Rest your forearm on a bench or thigh holding a dumbbell loaded at one end only. Rotate your wrist to turn the palm up (supination) and then down (pronation) under control. This trains the forearm rotators directly, so use a light load and a full range.',
  'Gripper Walks': 'Hold a hand gripper and squeeze it closed, then walk your fingers around the handles while maintaining tension. Keep the gripper under constant load throughout the movement. This builds crushing grip strength and finger endurance, so use a manageable resistance.',
  'Landmine Press (Abs)': 'Hold the end of the barbell at chest height and stand tall facing away from or towards the pivot. Press the bar up and forward while bracing hard against the rotational pull. The anti-rotation demand makes this as much a core exercise as a pressing one, so resist any twist.',
  'Half-Kneeling Pallof Press': 'Set a cable at chest height and kneel in a half-kneeling stance perpendicular to the pulley. Hold the handle at your chest, then press it straight out and resist the cable trying to rotate you. Brace your core hard and stay perfectly still through the torso.',
  'Tall-Kneeling Pallof Press': 'Kneel upright on both knees perpendicular to a chest-height cable. Press the handle straight out from your chest and resist the cable\'s pull to rotate you. The kneeling stance removes leg drive and forces the core to do all the anti-rotation work.',
  'GHD Sit-Up': 'Anchor your feet and ankles in the glute-ham developer and sit upright with your hips at the pad edge. Lower your torso back until you feel a stretch, then contract your abs to sit back up. Control the range carefully, as the extended position is demanding on the lower back.',
  'Incline Board Sit-Up': 'Anchor your feet at the top of a decline or incline sit-up board and lie back. Curl your ribcage towards your pelvis to sit up, then lower under control to a full stretch. Avoid pulling on your neck and let the abs do the work.',
  'Hanging Oblique Raise': 'Hang from a pull-up bar and raise your knees up and towards one side rather than straight ahead. Bring your knees towards the same-side armpit to load the obliques, then lower with control. Avoid swinging and alternate sides for balanced work.',
  'Seated Twist (Plate)': 'Sit on the floor with knees bent and torso leaning back slightly, holding a plate at your chest. Rotate your torso to touch the plate down to each side in turn. Rotate through the trunk, keep your lower back neutral, and avoid simply swinging the plate.',
  'Hip Adduction Machine': 'Sit with the pads against the insides of your knees and your legs spread apart. Squeeze your legs together against the resistance, then return slowly to a comfortable stretch. Avoid using momentum and control the eccentric to load the inner thighs fully.',
  'Cable Hip Adduction': 'Attach an ankle strap to a low cable and stand side-on so the working leg is nearest the pulley. Pull that leg across your body against the resistance, squeezing the inner thigh. Hold a support for balance and control the return rather than letting the cable snap it back.',
  'Copenhagen Adduction': 'Lie on your side with your top leg resting on a bench at about knee or shin height. Raise your hips and lift the bottom leg up to meet the bench, supporting through the top leg. This is a demanding adductor exercise, so shorten the lever by supporting at the knee until strong.',
  'Cossack Squat': 'Take a wide stance and shift your weight onto one bent leg while the other stays straight with the toes up. Sit deep on the working side, keeping your chest tall and heel down, then push back to centre. Move smoothly and control the depth to protect the inner thigh and knee.',
  'Sumo Squat (Adductor Focus)': 'Take a wide stance with toes turned out and squat down while pushing your knees out hard to track your toes. Keep your chest tall and back flat as you sit deep between your legs. The wide stance and deep range place strong emphasis on the inner thighs and glutes.',
  'Side-Lying Adduction': 'Lie on your side with the bottom leg straight and the top leg bent and placed in front for support. Raise the bottom leg upward towards the ceiling, squeezing the inner thigh, then lower slowly. Keep the movement controlled and the working leg straight throughout.',
  'Lateral Lunge': 'Step out wide to one side and bend that knee while keeping the other leg straight, sitting your hips back. Keep your chest up and the working heel planted, then push back to the start. This trains the quads, glutes and adductors through a side-to-side movement.',
  'Plate-Loaded Incline Press': 'Adjust the seat so the handles line up with your upper chest and press the handles up and slightly inward. Extend without locking out, then lower with control to feel an upper chest stretch. Keep your shoulder blades retracted and your back against the pad.',
  'Plate-Loaded Chest Press': 'Set the seat so the handles sit at mid-chest height and grip them firmly. Press forward until your arms are nearly straight, then control the return until you feel a chest stretch. Keep your back against the pad and avoid shrugging your shoulders.',
  'Plate-Loaded Decline Press': 'Adjust the seat for a downward pressing angle so the handles align with your lower chest. Press forward and slightly down, then return under control to stretch the lower chest. Keep your shoulder blades retracted and squeeze the chest at full extension.',
  'Iso-Lateral Chest Press': 'Set the seat so the handles are at mid-chest height and press one arm at a time or both together. The independent arms expose strength imbalances, so match each side\'s effort. Keep your back against the pad and control the return to stretch the chest.',
  'Plate-Loaded Lat Pulldown': 'Sit with your thighs secured and grip the handles overhead with arms fully extended. Pull the handles down towards your upper chest, driving your elbows down and squeezing your lats. Control the return to a full stretch at the top of each rep.',
  'Iso-Lateral Front Pulldown': 'Grip the handles overhead and pull one arm at a time or both together towards your upper chest. The independent arms let you work each lat through a full range and even out imbalances. Drive your elbows down and back, and control the stretch at the top.',
  'Plate-Loaded Row': 'Set your chest against the pad and grip the handles with arms extended. Pull the handles back towards your torso, driving your elbows back and squeezing your shoulder blades together. The chest support removes lower back strain, so focus purely on the back.',
  'Plate-Loaded High Row': 'Sit with your chest against the pad and grip the high handles overhead. Pull down and back towards your upper chest, driving your elbows towards your hips and squeezing the lats. Control the return to a full stretch at the top.',
  'Plate-Loaded Low Row': 'Set your chest against the pad and grip the low handles with arms extended forward. Pull the handles back towards your waist, driving your elbows behind you and squeezing your shoulder blades. Let your arms extend fully on the return for a complete lat stretch.',
  // Legacy/renamed name still present in older installs (now "Plate-Loaded Low Row").
  'Plate-Loaded Seated Row': 'Sit with your chest against the pad and grip the handles with your arms extended forward. Pull the handles back towards your waist, driving your elbows behind you and squeezing your shoulder blades together. Keep your torso upright and let your arms extend fully on the return for a complete lat stretch.',
  'Plate-Loaded Shoulder Press': 'Adjust the seat so the handles sit at or just below shoulder height. Press upward until your arms are nearly extended, then lower with control. Keep your back against the pad and your core braced to avoid arching your lower back.',
  'Plate-Loaded Rear Delt': 'Sit facing the pad and grip the handles in front of you at about shoulder height. Sweep your arms out and back in a wide arc, squeezing the rear delts and upper back. Keep the movement in your arms and avoid using your back to heave the weight.',
  'Plate-Loaded Preacher Curl': 'Rest your upper arms on the pad and grip the handles with your arms supported. Curl from full extension to a strong peak contraction, then lower under control. Be careful near full extension, as the bicep tendon is vulnerable in the stretched position.',
  'Plate-Loaded Hip Thrust': 'Set your upper back against the pad and position your feet so your shins are vertical at the top. Drive your hips up until your thighs are parallel to the floor, squeezing your glutes hard. Lower with control and avoid arching your lower back to finish the rep.',
  'Overhead Cable Rope Extension': 'Set a rope on a high cable, face away, and hold the rope overhead with your elbows bent. Extend your arms fully overhead, spreading the rope slightly at the top to squeeze the triceps. The overhead position maximises the stretch and load on the long head.',
  'Plate-Loaded Overhead Extension': 'Sit in the machine with the handles positioned behind your head and your elbows bent. Extend your arms overhead until straight, squeezing the triceps at the top. Keep your upper arms still and control the return to stretch the long head.',
  'Seated Dip Machine': 'Sit upright with your back against the pad and grip the handles at your sides. Press down until your arms are nearly straight, squeezing the triceps, then return with control. Keep your torso upright throughout to keep the focus on the triceps.',
  'Lateral Raise Machine': 'Sit with the pads against your forearms or elbows rather than your hands. Raise your arms out to the sides in a smooth arc until roughly parallel with the floor. Using the elbows reduces wrist involvement and keeps tension on the side delts, so lower slowly.',
  'Preacher Curl Machine': 'Rest your upper arms on the pad and grip the handles with your elbows aligned to the pivot. Curl to a full peak contraction, then lower to a complete stretch under control. The machine removes the need to balance the load, so focus on squeezing and controlling each rep.',
  'Triceps Extension Machine': 'Sit with your elbows aligned to the machine pivot and grip the handles. Push through the full range from bent to fully extended arms, squeezing the triceps. Lower with control and feel the stretch before the next rep.',
  'Glute Kickback Machine': 'Position yourself in the machine with the foot pad against your sole and your torso supported. Drive the working leg back and up, squeezing the glute hard at full extension. Avoid arching your lower back and control the leg back to the start.',
  'Ab Crunch Machine': 'Sit with your back against the pad and grip the handles or place your arms over the pads. Crunch your ribcage towards your pelvis by flexing your spine, not just pulling with your arms. Squeeze at the peak and lower under control without letting the weight stack bounce.',
  'Assisted Dip Machine': 'Kneel or stand on the assistance platform and grip the dip handles with your arms straight. Lower your body until your upper arms are roughly parallel to the floor, then press back up. The counterweight assists you, so reduce the assistance as you get stronger.',

  // EXPANSION (D8 / plan-A Option B, 2026-07-09): bands
  'Band Pull-Apart': 'Hold a band with both hands at shoulder height, arms straight out in front of you. Pull the band apart by drawing your hands out to the sides, squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end. Return with control rather than letting the band snap your arms back in.',
  'Band Face Pull': 'Anchor a band at chest height and hold an end in each hand with arms extended. Pull the band towards your face, flaring your elbows out wide until your hands finish beside your ears. Focus on rotating your shoulders outward at the end of each rep.',
  'Band Lateral Raise': 'Stand on the band with one end in each hand, or anchor it low to one side. Raise your arm out to the side until roughly parallel with the floor, leading with your elbow. Lower slowly, keeping the tension on throughout rather than letting the band go slack.',
  'Band Row': 'Anchor the band at chest height and step back until there is tension, holding an end in each hand. Pull your hands towards your ribs, driving your elbows back and squeezing your shoulder blades together. Let your arms extend fully on the return for a complete stretch.',
  'Band Lat Pulldown': 'Anchor the band overhead and kneel or sit facing it, holding an end in each hand. Pull your hands down towards your chest, driving your elbows down and back as if performing a pulldown. Control the return to a full overhead stretch each rep.',
  'Band Assisted Pull-Up': 'Loop a band over the bar and place a knee or foot in the loop for support. Perform the pull-up as normal, letting the band take some of your bodyweight through the bottom of the range. Reduce the assistance from the band over time as your pull-up strength improves.',
  'Band Chest Press': 'Anchor the band behind you at chest height and step forward until there is tension. Press your hands forward until your arms are extended, squeezing your chest at the end. Control the return, keeping the band under tension throughout.',
  'Band Bicep Curl': 'Stand on the band with feet shoulder-width apart, holding an end in each hand. Curl your hands up towards your shoulders, keeping your elbows pinned to your sides. Lower with control rather than letting the band pull your arms straight quickly.',
  'Band Tricep Pushdown': 'Anchor the band overhead and face away, holding an end in each hand with elbows bent. Push your hands down until your arms are straight, squeezing the triceps at the bottom. Keep your elbows tucked in and control the return to the start.',
  'Band Squat': 'Stand on the band with feet shoulder-width apart and hold the ends at your shoulders like a front squat. Squat down, keeping your chest tall and knees tracking your toes, then drive back up against the increasing band tension. The resistance grows through the top of the rep, so keep driving hard to lockout.',
  'Band Good Morning': 'Stand on the band with feet hip-width apart and the band across your upper back. Hinge at the hips, keeping a neutral spine, until you feel a stretch in your hamstrings. Drive your hips forward to return to standing, keeping the band taut throughout.',
  'Band Pull-Through': 'Anchor the band low behind you and step forward to face away, holding both ends between your legs. Hinge at the hips, letting the band pull back, then drive your hips forward to standing, squeezing your glutes and hamstrings. Keep a neutral spine throughout the hinge.',
  'Band Overhead Tricep Extension': 'Stand on the band and hold the other end behind your head with both hands, elbows bent and pointing up. Extend your arms overhead until straight, squeezing the triceps without flaring your elbows out. Lower with control to a full stretch behind your head.',
  'Banded Lateral Walk': 'Place a small loop band around your ankles or just above your knees and drop into a slight squat. Step sideways, keeping tension on the band and your knees pushed outward against it. Take controlled steps in one direction, then repeat in the other.',
  'Band Deadlift': 'Stand on the band with feet hip-width apart, holding the ends with a flat back and hips pushed back. Drive through your feet to stand tall, squeezing your glutes and hamstrings at the top. Lower under control, hinging back at the hips rather than rounding your spine.',
  'Band Tibialis Raise': 'Sit with your leg extended and loop the band around your foot, anchoring the other end under your other foot or a fixed point. Pull your toes up towards your shin against the band, then lower with control. This is a small range of motion, so focus on a full contraction at the top.',
  'Band Wrist Curl': 'Sit with your forearm supported on your thigh, holding the band under your foot or anchored below. Curl your wrist upward against the resistance, then lower with control to a full stretch. Keep the movement isolated to the wrist rather than swinging your arm.',
  'Band Wrist Extension': 'Sit with your forearm supported, palm facing down, holding the band anchored below. Lift the back of your hand upward against the resistance, then lower with control. This works the smaller opposing muscles that wrist curls miss, so keep the load light.',
  'Band Hip Thrust': 'Sit on the floor with your upper back against a bench and the band looped across your hips, anchored either side. Drive your hips up against the band, squeezing your glutes hard at the top. Lower with control and avoid arching your lower back to finish the rep.',
  'Band Leg Curl': 'Lie face down with the band anchored in front of you and looped around one ankle. Curl your heel towards your glute against the resistance, then lower with control. Keep your hips pressed into the floor throughout so the hamstring does the work.',
  'Band Shoulder Press': 'Stand on the band with feet shoulder-width apart, holding the ends at shoulder height. Press your hands overhead until your arms are extended, then lower with control back to shoulder height. Keep your core braced so the pull of the band does not arch your lower back.',

  // EXPANSION: hamstrings hip-extension fill
  'Bodyweight Single-Leg RDL': 'Stand on one leg with a soft bend in the knee, hinging forward at the hips as your other leg extends back for balance. Keep the knee of your standing leg soft and your back flat, lowering until your torso is roughly parallel to the floor. Drive your hips forward to return upright, using a wall or chair for balance if you need it.',
  'Glute-Ham Raise Machine': 'Position your knees on the pad and hook your heels under the footplate, with your hips supported over the hinge point. Lower your torso forward under control, feeling the hamstrings lengthen, then drive your hips back to the start. Keep the movement smooth rather than dropping quickly into the stretch.',

  // EXPANSION: rear delts
  'Prone Reverse Fly': 'Lie face down on a raised surface such as an incline bench, arms hanging straight down. Raise your arms out to the sides in a wide arc, squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top, like a superman with a Y shape. Lower with control back to the hang position.',

  // EXPANSION: chest
  'Incline Push-Up (Hands Elevated)': 'Place your hands on a bench, step or box, with your body in a straight line from head to heels. Lower your chest towards your hands, keeping your elbows at roughly 45 degrees, then press back up. The elevated hand position shifts more emphasis onto the upper chest.',

  // EXPANSION: front delts
  'Machine Shoulder Press (Front Delt Focus)': 'Adjust the seat so the handles sit level with your shoulders and grip with palms facing forward. Press upward until your arms are nearly straight, then lower with control back to shoulder height. The fixed path removes the need to balance the weight, so focus on driving through the front delts.',

  // EXPANSION: calves
  'Seated Bodyweight Calf Raise': 'Sit with the balls of your feet on a raised edge or step and your knees bent. Press through your toes to raise your heels as high as possible, then lower until you feel a deep stretch. This bent-knee position emphasises the soleus, the muscle underneath the main calf.',

  // EXPANSION: abs / rotation
  'Oblique V-Up': 'Lie on your side with your legs stacked and one arm supporting your head. Lift your legs and torso together in a small crunch to one side, bringing your hip and shoulder closer together. Lower with control and complete all reps on one side before switching.',
  'Cable Reverse Woodchop (Kneeling)': 'Kneel side-on to a low cable pulley and hold the handle with both hands. Rotate your torso to pull the handle diagonally up and across your body, away from the machine. Control the return, keeping the movement in your trunk rather than your arms.',
  'Weighted Russian Twist (Medicine Ball)': 'Sit with your knees bent and lean back slightly, holding a medicine ball or plate at your chest. Rotate your torso to tap the weight down on each side in turn, keeping your feet either on the floor or lifted for a harder variation. Rotate through your core and control the speed rather than swinging.',
  'Cable Woodchop (Half-Kneeling)': 'Kneel on one knee side-on to a high cable pulley, with your other foot planted out to the side. Pull the handle down and across your body in a chopping motion, rotating through your torso. Control the return to the start position and complete all reps before switching sides.',
  'Single-Arm Cable Woodchop (Standing)': 'Stand side-on to a cable set at chest height and hold the handle with one hand. Rotate your torso to pull the handle across your body, keeping your arm relatively straight and letting your core drive the movement. Control the return and complete all reps on one side before switching.',

  // EXPANSION: unilateral/symmetry depth
  'Single-Arm Cable Lateral Raise': 'Stand side-on to a low cable pulley and hold the handle in the hand furthest from the machine. Raise your arm out to the side until roughly parallel with the floor, leading with your elbow. Lower slowly under the constant cable tension before repeating on the other side.',
  'Single-Arm Cable Rear Delt Fly': 'Stand facing a cable set at chest height and take the handle with the opposite hand, crossing your body. Pull your arm out and back in a wide arc, squeezing your rear delt at the end. Control the return and complete all reps before switching sides.',
  'Single-Arm Dumbbell Shrug': 'Hold a dumbbell in one hand at your side, with the other hand free or resting on a support. Shrug your shoulder straight up towards your ear, squeezing at the top, then lower with control. Working one side at a time helps you feel and correct any imbalance between shoulders.',
  'Single-Arm Cable Wrist Curl': 'Kneel or sit with your forearm supported on a bench, holding a low cable handle with your palm facing up. Curl your wrist upward against the resistance, then lower to a full stretch. Keep the movement isolated to the wrist and complete all reps on one side before switching.',
  'Single-Leg Tibialis Raise': 'Stand on one leg with your heel planted and the other leg lifted slightly. Pull your toes up towards your shin as high as you can, then lower with control. This small range of motion trains the shin muscle that balances out calf work, so complete all reps before switching legs.',
  'Single-Arm Farmer Carry': 'Hold a single dumbbell or kettlebell at your side, standing tall with your shoulders level. Walk forward for the set distance or time, resisting the urge to lean towards the loaded side. This anti-lateral-flexion demand makes it a genuine core exercise as well as a grip and trap builder.',

  // EXPANSION: adductors
  'Adductor Squeeze (Ball)': 'Lie on your back with your knees bent and a ball or cushion held firmly between your knees. Squeeze the ball as hard as you can and hold for the set time, keeping your hips still. This is a simple, joint-friendly way to train the inner thighs with no equipment.',
  'Wide-Stance Goblet Squat (Adductor Bias)': 'Hold a dumbbell vertically at your chest and take a wider stance than a normal goblet squat, toes turned out. Squat down between your legs, pushing your knees out to track your toes, then drive back up. The wide stance and turned-out toes shift more of the work onto the inner thighs.',
  'Zercher Sumo Squat (Adductor Focus)': 'Cradle a barbell in the crooks of your elbows and take a wide sumo stance with toes turned out. Squat down, keeping your chest tall and knees pushing out over your toes, then drive back up. The front-loaded hold and wide stance combine to challenge the adductors and upper back together.',
  'Adductor Rock-Back (Kneeling)': 'Kneel with one leg out to the side, straight, and the other bent underneath you. Rock your weight sideways over the straight leg, feeling a stretch and contraction through the inner thigh, then return to centre. Move slowly and keep the motion controlled rather than bouncing.',

  // EXPANSION: tibialis
  'Dumbbell Tibialis Raise (Seated)': 'Sit with a dumbbell resting on top of your foot, heel planted on the floor. Pull your toes up towards your shin against the added load, then lower with control. Keep the range small and controlled, as this is a precise, low-load movement.',
  'Tib Bar Raise (Machine)': 'Sit or stand at the machine with the pad resting across the top of your foot, heel planted. Pull your toes up against the resistance as high as you can, then lower under control. This isolates the shin muscle that balances out heavy calf training.',

  // EXPANSION: neck
  'Plate Neck Lateral Flexion': 'Sit or stand tall and hold a light plate against the side of your head with one hand for light support only. Tilt your head towards your shoulder against gentle resistance, then return to upright. Use very light load and slow, controlled reps, as the neck is a small, delicate muscle group.',
  'Neck Machine Lateral Flexion': 'Sit with the side of your head against the machine pad, positioned so the pivot lines up with your neck. Tilt your head sideways against the resistance, then return to upright under control. Start with a light setting and build up gradually.',

  // EXPANSION: forearms
  'Behind-the-Back Wrist Curl (Barbell)': 'Stand holding a barbell behind your back with an underhand grip, arms hanging straight down. Curl your wrists upward, lifting the bar as high as you can, then lower to a full stretch. Keep your arms still throughout so the movement stays isolated to the wrists.',
  'Wrist Roller': 'Hold the wrist roller device in front of you with arms extended, weight hanging from the cord. Rotate your wrists to wind the cord up, raising the weight, then reverse to lower it under control. Keep your arms level and let your forearms do all the work.',

  // EXPANSION: traps
  'Kettlebell Shrug': 'Hold a kettlebell in each hand, or one held with both hands, standing tall with arms straight. Shrug your shoulders straight up towards your ears, squeezing at the top, then lower with control. Avoid rolling your shoulders, keep the movement a straight up-and-down shrug.',
  'Cable Behind-the-Back Shrug': 'Stand facing away from a low cable pulley, holding the handle behind your back with arms straight. Shrug your shoulders straight up, squeezing at the top, then lower under control. The behind-the-back position keeps the load in a strong line for the traps.',

  // EXPANSION: side / front / rear delts
  'Cable Y-Raise (Standing)': 'Stand facing away from a low cable pulley, holding the handle with one arm extended down and across your body. Raise your arm up and out into a Y shape above your head, keeping a slight bend in the elbow. Lower with control and complete all reps before switching sides.',
  'Egyptian Lateral Raise': 'Stand side-on to a support, holding it with one hand and leaning your torso away from the working arm. Raise the dumbbell out to the side, leading with your elbow, using the lean to extend the range of motion. Lower slowly and complete all reps before switching sides.',
  'Landmine Front Raise': 'Stand facing a landmine attachment and hold the end of the barbell with both hands in front of your thighs. Raise the bar up to shoulder height, keeping your arms mostly straight, then lower with control. The fixed arc keeps tension on the front delts throughout.',
  'Landmine Rear Delt Row': 'Stand over a landmine attachment, hinged forward at the hips, and hold the end of the bar with both hands. Row the bar up towards your chest, flaring your elbows out wide to bias the rear delts. Lower with control to a full stretch each rep.',
  'Machine Y-Raise': 'Sit facing the machine and grip the handles with your arms extended down and together in front of you. Raise your arms up and out into a Y shape, squeezing your rear delts and upper back at the top. Lower with control back to the start.',
  'Cuban Press': 'Hold dumbbells at your sides and raise your elbows out to shoulder height, upper arms parallel to the floor. Rotate your forearms upward so the dumbbells point overhead, then press straight up to lockout. Reverse the sequence on the way down, this trains the rotator cuff and all three delt heads together.',
  'Bradford Press': 'Hold a barbell at shoulder height and press it just above your head, stopping short of a full overhead lockout. Lower the bar behind your head to the base of your neck, then press it back up and forward to the front. Keep the reps smooth and controlled, as the constant tension is the point of the exercise.',
  'Cable Lateral Raise (Behind the Back)': 'Stand with a low cable pulley behind you, holding the handle with the hand on the same side, arm across your back. Raise your arm out to the side and up until roughly parallel with the floor. This angle keeps tension on the side delt from a different line of pull than a standard raise.',
  'Reverse Cable Crossover': 'Stand between two high cable pulleys, crossing your arms in front of you to take the opposite handles. Pull your hands out and back in a wide arc, squeezing your rear delts and upper back. Control the return to the crossed start position each rep.',

  // EXPANSION: calves
  'Seated Calf Raise (Barbell)': 'Sit with a barbell resting across your knees, just above the joint, and the balls of your feet on a raised block. Press through your toes to raise your heels as high as possible, then lower to a deep stretch. Keep the bar steady with your hands throughout.',
  'Donkey Calf Raise (Machine)': 'Bend forward into the machine with your hips against the pad and the balls of your feet on the platform. Press through your toes to raise your heels as high as possible, then lower to a full stretch. The bent-over position loads the calf through a longer range than a standing raise.',
  'Eccentric Calf Raise (Bodyweight)': 'Rise onto your toes using both feet on a raised edge, then shift your weight onto one leg. Lower that heel down as slowly as you can, taking several seconds to reach the bottom stretch. Use both feet to rise back up, then repeat, the slow lowering is where the real work happens.',

  // EXPANSION: quads
  'Sissy Squat Machine': 'Position your shins against the pad and hold the handles for support, knees at the pivot point. Lean back and bend your knees to lower your torso, feeling a deep stretch through the front of the thigh, then drive back up. Keep your hips extended throughout so the quads take the load, not the hips.',
  'Belt Squat': 'Attach the belt around your hips and stand on the platform with feet shoulder-width apart. Squat down, keeping your chest tall and knees tracking your toes, then drive back up through your heels. The load hangs from your hips rather than your spine, so it is a good option when you want quad work without axial loading.',
  'Reverse Nordic Curl': 'Kneel with your hips locked forward and something securing your feet, arms free at your sides. Lean back slowly from the knees only, keeping your hips extended, until you feel a strong stretch through the front of your thighs. Use your quads to pull yourself back to upright, controlling the movement throughout.',
  'Smith Machine Front Squat': 'Rest the bar across the front of your shoulders, elbows lifted high to create a shelf. Squat down keeping your torso upright and knees tracking forward, then drive back up. The fixed bar path removes the need to balance the bar, so focus on depth and knee tracking.',
  'Bodyweight Bulgarian Split Squat': 'Rest the top of one foot on a bench behind you and step your front foot far enough forward to keep your knee stacked over your ankle. Lower your back knee towards the floor, keeping your chest tall, then drive back up through your front foot. This is a demanding no-equipment single-leg option, so use a wall or chair for balance if needed.',
  'Wall Ball Squat': 'Hold a dumbbell or medicine ball at your chest and stand with feet shoulder-width apart. Squat down until your thighs are at least parallel to the floor, then drive up explosively. Keep your chest tall and knees tracking your toes throughout.',
  'Cable Squat (Standing)': 'Stand facing away from a low cable pulley, holding the handle at your chest with both hands. Squat down, keeping your chest tall and knees tracking your toes, using the cable for a counterbalance. Drive back up through your heels, keeping tension on the cable throughout.',

  // EXPANSION: hamstrings
  'Kettlebell Romanian Deadlift': 'Hold a kettlebell (or two) in front of your thighs and hinge at the hips, pushing your hips back. Keep a neutral spine and lower the kettlebell down your shins until you feel a strong hamstring stretch. Drive your hips forward to return to standing, squeezing your glutes at the top.',
  'Slider Leg Curl (Bodyweight)': 'Lie on your back with your heels on sliders or a towel on a smooth floor, hips raised. Bend your knees to slide your heels towards your glutes, keeping your hips lifted throughout. Extend your legs back out under control, this trains the hamstrings without any equipment.',
  'Landmine Romanian Deadlift': 'Stand over a landmine attachment and hold the end of the bar with both hands in front of your thighs. Hinge at the hips, pushing them back and keeping a neutral spine, until you feel a hamstring stretch. Drive your hips forward to return upright, squeezing your glutes at the top.',

  // EXPANSION: glutes
  'B-Stance Hip Thrust': 'Set your upper back against a bench and position one foot flat with the other only lightly touching the floor for balance. Drive through the flat foot to lift your hips, squeezing the glute on that side hard at the top. Lower with control and complete all reps before switching your lead foot.',
  'Deficit Reverse Lunge (Glute Focus)': 'Stand on a low step or plate and step one foot back and down into a reverse lunge, lowering your back knee towards the floor. Push your hips back slightly as you descend to bias the glutes over the quads. Drive through your front heel to return to standing on the step.',
  'Weighted Frog Pump': 'Lie on your back with the soles of your feet together and knees dropped out to the sides, a dumbbell resting on your hips. Drive your hips up, squeezing your glutes hard at the top, then lower under control. Hold the dumbbell steady with your hands throughout the set.',

  // EXPANSION: chest
  'Landmine Chest Press (Single-Arm)': 'Stand or kneel facing away from a landmine attachment, holding the end of the bar at chest height with one hand. Press the bar forward along its natural arc until your arm is extended, then lower with control. The single-arm hold challenges your core to resist rotation as you press.',
  'Deficit Push-Up': 'Place your hands on two low blocks or plates, wider than shoulder width, with your body in a straight line. Lower your chest below hand height for a deeper stretch than a standard push-up, then press back up. Keep your core braced throughout so your hips do not sag into the deficit.',
  'Cable Iron Cross': 'Stand centred between two low cable pulleys, holding a handle in each hand with arms out to the sides. Bring your hands up and together in front of your chest in a wide arc, squeezing at the top. Control the return to a full stretch across your chest.',
  'Incline Cable Chest Press': 'Set the cables low and sit or stand on an incline bench angled towards the machine. Press your hands up and forward along the incline angle until your arms are extended, then control the return. Keep your shoulder blades retracted throughout the press.',
  'Floor Press (Dumbbell)': 'Lie on the floor with knees bent and dumbbells held at your chest, upper arms resting on the floor at the bottom. Press the dumbbells straight up until your arms are extended, then lower until your upper arms touch the floor. The floor stops the descent early, which is easier on the shoulders than a full-range bench press.',
  'Push-Up Plus': 'Get into a push-up position with arms extended and your body in a straight line. Without bending your elbows, push your upper back up towards the ceiling, protracting your shoulder blades apart. Return to the neutral start position, this small extra movement trains the serratus anterior alongside the chest.',
  'Chest Press Machine (Single-Arm)': 'Sit with your back against the pad and grip one handle at mid-chest height, keeping the other hand resting or braced. Press forward until your arm is nearly straight, then control the return to a chest stretch. Working one side at a time helps even out any strength imbalance between arms.',
  'Machine Chest Fly (Single-Arm)': 'Sit with your back against the pad and grip one handle at chest height, arm slightly bent. Bring your hand across your body in a controlled arc, squeezing your chest at the finish. Control the return to a full stretch and complete all reps before switching arms.',
  'Standing Cable Chest Press (Single-Arm)': 'Stand in a staggered stance facing away from a cable set at chest height, holding the handle in one hand. Press your hand forward until your arm is extended, resisting any rotation through your torso. Control the return and complete all reps before switching sides.',

  // EXPANSION: back
  'Batwing Row': 'Lie face down on a high incline bench holding a dumbbell in each hand, arms hanging straight down. Row both dumbbells up towards your hips, squeezing your shoulder blades together, then hold briefly at the top before lowering. The chest support removes momentum entirely, so every rep is strict.',
  'Wide-Grip Pull-Up': 'Take a grip wider than shoulder width on the bar and hang with arms fully extended. Pull yourself up until your chin clears the bar, driving your elbows down and out to the sides. Lower with control, the wider grip shifts more emphasis onto the upper back and lats.',
  'Cable Reverse-Grip Pulldown': 'Sit with your thighs secured and take an underhand, shoulder-width grip on the bar. Pull the bar down to your upper chest, driving your elbows down and back, and squeezing your lats and biceps. Control the return to a full stretch at the top.',
  'Renegade Row': 'Start in a push-up position with a dumbbell in each hand, feet set wide for stability. Row one dumbbell up towards your hip while bracing hard through your core to resist rotating, then lower and repeat on the other side. Keep your hips as still as possible throughout each row.',
  'Cable Rope Straight-Arm Pulldown (Single-Arm)': 'Stand facing a high cable holding a single handle in one hand, arm extended above you. Keeping your arm mostly straight, pull the handle down to your thigh, feeling your lat contract. Control the return and complete all reps before switching sides.',
  'Single-Arm Landmine Row': 'Stand over a landmine attachment, hinged forward at the hips, and grip the end of the bar with one hand. Row the bar up towards your hip, driving your elbow back and squeezing your lat. Lower with control to a full stretch and complete all reps before switching sides.',

  // EXPANSION: biceps
  'Cable Concentration Curl': 'Sit facing a low cable pulley and brace your working elbow against the inside of your thigh. Curl the handle up towards your shoulder, squeezing the bicep hard at the top. Lower under control to a full stretch, keeping your upper arm pinned in place throughout.',
  'EZ Bar Drag Curl': 'Hold an EZ bar with a shoulder-width grip and let your elbows drift back as you curl, dragging the bar up along your torso. This keeps the bar path close to your body rather than swinging forward, biasing the bicep peak. Lower with the same dragging path under control.',
  'Zottman Preacher Curl': 'Rest your upper arms on a preacher pad and curl a dumbbell up with your palm facing up, as normal. At the top, rotate your wrist so your palm faces down, then lower slowly in that reversed grip. The pronated lowering trains your forearms as well as your biceps.',
  'Incline Hammer Curl': 'Sit on an incline bench with dumbbells hanging straight down, palms facing your body. Curl the dumbbells up while keeping your palms facing each other throughout, without rotating your wrists. The incline position stretches the long head of the bicep at the bottom of each rep.',
  'Cable Overhead Bicep Curl': 'Stand or kneel between two high cable pulleys, arms raised out to the sides at shoulder height. Curl your hands towards your head, keeping your upper arms still and elevated. This unusual angle keeps constant tension on the biceps through a shortened range.',

  // EXPANSION: triceps
  'Dumbbell Floor Skull Crusher': 'Lie on the floor with knees bent, holding dumbbells above your chest with arms extended. Bend your elbows to lower the dumbbells towards your head, keeping your upper arms still, then extend back up. The floor limits the range slightly, which keeps the elbows in a safer position than a bench version.',
  'Single-Arm Overhead Cable Extension': 'Stand facing away from a high cable, holding the handle overhead in one hand with your elbow bent. Extend your arm fully overhead, keeping your upper arm close to your head, then lower with control. Complete all reps on one side before switching, this exposes and corrects any strength difference between arms.',
  'Bench Press (Close Grip, Dumbbell)': 'Lie on a bench holding dumbbells close together above your chest, palms facing each other. Lower the dumbbells to your sides, keeping your elbows tucked in close to your body, then press back up. The close elbow path shifts more of the load onto the triceps than a standard press.',
  'Landmine Tricep Extension': 'Kneel facing a landmine attachment and hold the end of the bar close to your forehead with both hands. Extend your arms forward and up along the arc of the bar until straight, then lower with control. Keep your upper arms still so the triceps do the work.',
  'Cross-Body Cable Tricep Extension': 'Stand side-on to a low cable pulley, holding the handle in the far hand across your body. Extend your arm down and across your body until straight, squeezing the tricep at the bottom. Control the return and complete all reps before switching sides.',

  // EXPANSION: abs
  'Weighted Plank (Plate on Back)': 'Get into a standard plank position on your forearms, with a light plate placed flat across your upper back. Hold a straight line from head to heels for the set time, bracing your core hard. Only add load once a long bodyweight plank feels easy, the extra weight adds up quickly.',
  'Cable Anti-Rotation Hold (Half-Kneeling)': 'Kneel in a half-kneeling stance side-on to a chest-height cable, holding the handle at your chest with both hands. Press the handle straight out and hold, resisting the pull of the cable to rotate your torso. Keep completely still through the trunk for the set time before switching sides.',
  'Reverse Plank': 'Sit with your legs extended and hands planted behind your hips, fingers pointing towards your feet. Push through your hands and heels to lift your hips up until your body forms a straight line facing the ceiling. Hold the position, keeping your hips up and your core braced throughout.',
};
